#proof[
  The projective quotient $S$ in Lemma 78 is a point: otherwise its rational functions pull back nontrivially to
    $W$. Thus $M tilde.op_(bb(Q)) 0$ and $H tilde.op_(bb(Q)) K_W + T$.
    By Lemma 79 and $cal(G)_(dim W)$, its pullback to a smooth higher model is a semiample rational line plus its
    negative divisor. Every semiample line on a space of algebraic dimension zero is torsion,
    since its generated system has zero-dimensional image. Proposition 11,
    applied to this modification with zero added divisor, therefore gives Equation (5.7),
    including rationality of the divisor. ◻
]

=== 5.3 Projective programs under the assumption <rank:projective-program-input>

The argument for a projective base uses a terminating program to reach a nef adjoint and,
  after decreasing the nef data, a program ending in a Mori fiber space.
  The conditional input is termination in the pseudo-effective case.
  We first state the exact program result and then derive that case from the projective good models in Proposition 12.

#thm([Proposition 5.5.])[
  (A conditional generalized program).  #emph[Assume Assumption 1.
    Let $\( V \, B + bold(M) \)$ be a projective generalized dlt rational pair, where $V$ is $bb(Q)$-factorial,
    $B gt.eq 0$, and the nef b-divisor $bold(M)$ is determined by a nef rational Cartier divisor on a projective
    birational model. Put $D = K_V + B + M_V$.]
  #emph[There is a choice of $D$-MMP with ample scaling which terminates.
    If $D$ is pseudo-effective, its endpoint $V_m$ has nef adjoint $D_m$.
    If $D$ is not pseudo-effective, its endpoint has a Mori fiber contraction to a projective variety of smaller
    dimension. The birational steps and their endpoints stay $bb(Q)$-factorial generalized dlt,
    and the forward birational map extracts no divisors. In the pseudo-effective case,
    on a common resolution one has an actual rational divisor identity
$ u^(*) D = v^(*) D_m + F \, wide F gt.eq 0 upright(" and ") v upright("-exceptional") . $

]
] <proj:generalized-mmp>

For the pseudo-effective alternative, we first derive the relative weak Zariski decompositions used by the
  minimal-model existence theorem.

For a projective morphism, first replace its image by its normal Stein factor.
  We use the relative convention of #cite(label("TsakanikasXie")) Section 2:
  a divisor is pseudo-effective over the base when its restriction to a very general fiber of this surjective
  morphism is pseudo-effective. An NQC weak Zariski decomposition of $D$ over the base is a birational equality
  $h^(*) D equiv_Z P + N$, where $P$ is a nonnegative real combination of relatively nef rational Cartier divisors
  and $N gt.eq 0$. The decomposition below has rational Cartier parts.

#thm([Lemma 5.6.])[
  (Relative canonical decomposition).  #emph[Assume Assumption 1.
    Let $V$ be a smooth quasi-projective complex variety and let $f : V arrow.r Z$ be projective,
    with $Z$ normal and quasi-projective. If $K_V$ is pseudo-effective over $Z$,
    then on a normal variety $Y$ with a projective birational morphism $h : Y arrow.r
    V$ there are rational Cartier divisors $P \, N$ such that
$ h^(*) K_V equiv_Z P + N \, wide P upright(" nef over ") Z \, wide N gt.eq 0 . $

]
] <proj:relative-wzd>

#proof[
  We use the construction in the proof of #cite(label("External037")) Proposition 3.3.
    Its absolute input is a nef-plus- effective rational decomposition for the canonical divisor of a smooth
    projective variety with pseudo-effective canonical class.
    This is supplied here by Proposition 12 and its common-resolution comparison.
  Replace the image of $f$ by its normal Stein factor. This does not change relative numerical classes or
    contracted curves, and now $f$ is surjective with connected fibers.
    Its very general fiber is smooth projective with pseudo-effective canonical divisor,
    hence is non-uniruled by #cite(label("BDPP")) Theorem 0.2 and Corollary 0.3.
    Compactify the projective morphism and resolve away from $V$.
    We obtain $macron(f) : macron(V) arrow.r macron(Z)$, with $macron(V)$ smooth projective and $macron(Z)$ normal
    projective, unchanged over $Z$. If $dim Z = 0$, $V$ is already projective and the absolute input proves the lemma.
  Suppose $dim Z > 0$, and let $r : tilde(Z) arrow.r macron(Z)$ be a smooth projective resolution.
    Choose a sufficiently positive very ample divisor $A_Z$ on $macron(Z)$ and a general $B in \| 2 A_Z \|$.
    The pulled-back systems on both $tilde(Z)$ and $macron(V)$ are base point free.
    Bertini makes $r^(*) B$ and $macron(f)^(*) B$ smooth nonempty reduced divisors. Their double covers
  $ pi_Z : Z^sharp arrow.r tilde(Z) \, wide pi : V^sharp arrow.r macron(V) $
  are smooth integral projective varieties. The canonical formulas are
  $ K_(Z^sharp) tilde.op_(bb(Q)) pi_Z^(*) \( K_(tilde(Z)) + r^(*) A_Z \) \, wide
    K_(V^sharp) tilde.op_(bb(Q)) pi^(*) \( K_(macron(V)) + macron(f)^(*) A_Z \) . $
  The first line is big when $A_Z$ is sufficiently positive.
    The rational map $V^sharp arrow.r.dashed Z^sharp$ has the same very general fibers as $f$.
    A covering family of rational curves on $V^sharp$ would either dominate a covering family of rational curves
    on $Z^sharp$, or cover its very general fibers by vertical rational curves. Both are impossible.
    Thus $V^sharp$ is non-uniruled and $K_(V^sharp)$ is pseudo-effective by BDPP.
  Apply the absolute decomposition to $V^sharp$. Moving the rational principal difference in the canonical formula
    into the nef part gives an actual rational divisor decomposition for a pullback of
    $pi^(*) \( K_(macron(V)) + macron(f)^(*) A_Z \)$. Take a common equivariant resolution of that model and its
    conjugate under the covering involution. On this smooth projective resolution $g : T arrow.r V^sharp$,
    average the two decompositions. We obtain
  $ g^(*) pi^(*) \( K_(macron(V)) + macron(f)^(*) A_Z \) = macron(P) + macron(N) \, wide
    macron(P) upright(" nef") \, quad macron(N) gt.eq 0 \, $
  where $macron(P)$ and $macron(N)$ are invariant rational Cartier divisors.
  Let $q : T arrow.r macron(Y)$ be the finite quotient by the involution.
    The invariant composite $pi g$ induces a projective birational morphism $macron(h) : macron(Y) arrow.r
    macron(V)$, with $macron(Y)$ normal and projective. An invariant rational divisor descends by dividing its
    coefficient at an upstairs prime by the ramification index.
    The descended divisor is rational Cartier when the original divisor is:
    after clearing denominators, choose one Cartier equation on a semilocal neighborhood of the finite orbit above
    a point (a Cartier divisor is principal on a semilocal ring), and multiply all its translates.
    The product is invariant and has divisor equal to the group order times the original divisor.
    It descends to a local equation for a multiple of the downstairs divisor.
    This argument includes stabilizers and ramification primes.
  Accordingly $macron(P) = q^(*) P$ and $macron(N) = q^(*) N$ for rational Cartier divisors on $macron(Y)$.
    Lifting curves through the finite map shows that $P$ is nef,
    and coefficientwise descent shows that $N$ is effective. Finite pullback is injective on rational divisors,
    so the displayed identity descends. Restrict it to $Y = macron(h)^(- 1) \( V \)$.
    The term pulled back from $A_Z$ is numerically trivial over $Z$, giving $h^(*) K_V equiv_Z P + N$, as required. ◻
]

#proof[
  Lemma 82 and #cite(label("TsakanikasXie")) Theorem 5.2, applied to the generalized pair $\( U \/ Z \, 0 + 0 \)$
    with $U$ smooth, prove relative minimal-model existence for smooth varieties by dimension induction.
    The hypothesis of that theorem is relative smooth minimal-model existence one dimension lower;
    the lemma supplies its other hypothesis, an NQC weak Zariski decomposition.
    The induction begins in dimension zero. Then #cite(label("TsakanikasXie")) Theorem 5.4 supplies minimal models
    for every pseudo-effective NQC generalized lc pair in the relevant dimension.
    Their Theorem 2.7 gives existence in the Birkar–Shokurov sense.
  The nef data in the statement are NQC, because a nef rational Cartier divisor is a positive multiple of a nef
    Cartier divisor. In the pseudo-effective case the preceding paragraph therefore gives the Birkar–Shokurov
    model required by #cite(label("TsakanikasXie")) Theorem 4.2.
    In the other case, the non-pseudo-effective alternative of that theorem applies directly.
    To meet its scaling hypothesis, take an effective sufficiently positive ample rational divisor $A$ whose
    general components have sufficiently small coefficients. On a fixed log resolution carrying $bold(M)$,
    these components are transverse to the boundary and preserve generalized log canonicity,
    while their ample class can be chosen large enough that $D + A$ is nef.
    The theorem supplies a terminating MMP starting on $V$.
  We verify the step types inductively. Suppose $V_i$ is $bb(Q)$-factorial generalized dlt.
    By #cite(label("TsakanikasXie")) Remark 2.15, each birational step has the form
  $ V_i arrow.r^(g_i) Z_i arrow.l^(h_i) V_(i + 1) \, $
  where $g_i$ contracts a negative extremal ray and $h_i$ is small and projective.
    Suppose $g_i$ is divisorial, and write $R$ for its ray. An exceptional prime $E$ has $E dot.op R < 0$:
    otherwise it would be effective, exceptional, and $g_i$-nef, contrary to negativity.
    There is only one exceptional prime. Indeed, a combination of two distinct exceptional primes can be chosen to
    have degree zero on $R$; negativity applied to both signs of that combination would make it zero.
  We show directly that $Z_i$ is $bb(Q)$-factorial. For a prime divisor $T$ on $Z_i$,
    let $tilde(T)$ be its strict transform, choose a curve $C$ spanning $R$, and put
  $ a = - frac(tilde(T) dot.op C, E dot.op C) in bb(Q) . $
  Choose a positive integer $m$ for which $m \( tilde(T) + a E \)$ is Cartier.
    Its line has degree zero on $R$, so #cite(label("Xie2022")) Theorem 1.5 gives an actual Cartier line
    $cal(L)_T$ on $Z_i$ and an isomorphism
  $ cal(O)_(V_i) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] m \( tilde(T) + a E \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] tilde.eq g_i^(*)
    cal(L)_T . $
  On an open set trivializing $cal(L)_T$, the Cartier divisor $m \( tilde(T) + a E \)$ is principal upstairs.
    Pushing this principal divisor identity down through the birational $g_i$ gives a principal divisor $m
    T$ on that open set. Thus $T$ is rational Cartier. It follows that $h_i$ is an isomorphism:
    the pushforward of an $h_i$-ample Cartier divisor is rational Cartier on $Z_i$,
    and its pullback is the original divisor because $h_i$ is small.
    It has degree zero on every contracted curve, so relative ampleness rules out positive-dimensional fibers;
    a finite birational map to the normal $Z_i$ is an isomorphism.
    If $g_i$ is small, the relatively ample canonical model $h_i$ is the flip of #cite(label("HanLi"))
    Definition 3.2. Thus the chosen birational steps are divisorial contractions and flips.
    The underlying variety of a generalized dlt pair is klt, and these steps preserve the $bb(Q)$-factorial
    generalized dlt category #cite(label("HanLi")) Remark 2.3 and Lemma 3.7.
    They introduce no prime divisors on the new models. For completeness,
    on a common resolution of one step $V_i arrow.r.dashed V_(i + 1)$ carrying $bold(M)$,
    taking the difference of the two generalized discrepancy formulas cancels their common nef divisor.
    The resulting difference of the adjoint pullbacks is exceptional over the new model and anti-nef over it.
    The negativity lemma makes this difference effective. Composing these actual rational divisor comparisons
    gives $u^(*) D = v^(*) D_m + F$ with $F$ effective and exceptional over the endpoint.
    The endpoints in the two cases are the nef and Mori fiber endpoints supplied by the terminating program theorem. ◻
]

=== 5.4 Reducing a projective base <reducing-a-projective-base>

The nef line $M$ supplied by the Hodge construction need not be semiample.
  A first projective program replaces $H$ by a nef transform.
  Positive Iitaka dimension gives either a smaller base or a big line.
  In nonpositive Iitaka dimension, rationally trivial nef data give a trivial line.
  In the remaining case, decreasing $M$ leads to a second program that lowers the base dimension while preserving
  the first nef line.

#thm([Lemma 5.7.])[
  (Projective base reduction).  #emph[Assume Assumption 1 and the setting of Lemma 79,
    with $W$ projective of dimension $k$. Then one of the following holds.]
  + #emph[After a smooth source modification with the reduced-exceptional boundary,
    there is a fibration to a smooth projective variety $V$ with $0 < dim V <
    k$ and logarithmic Kodaira dimension zero on very general smooth fibers.]
  + #emph[There are a normal $bb(Q)$-factorial projective variety $W_m$,
    a birational contraction $W arrow.r.dashed W_m$ extracting no divisors,
    and a nef rational line $H_m$ on $W_m$, either big or rationally trivial,
    such that on a common smooth resolution $mu : W' arrow.r W$, $nu : W' arrow.r W_m$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.8)")
 $ mu^(*) H tilde.op_(bb(Q)) nu^(*) H_m + E \, wide E gt.eq 0 med nu upright("-exceptional") . $ <rank:base-endpoint>]

]
  #emph[Here all the line comparisons are actual rational line identities.]
] <rank:base-reduction>

#proof[
  The smooth pair with boundary $T$ and nef data $bold(M)$ determined by $M$ on $W$ is a rational
    $bb(Q)$-factorial generalized dlt pair. Its adjoint $H$ is pseudo-effective by Lemma 79.
    Proposition 81 gives a chosen terminating program with scaling to a $bb(Q)$-factorial generalized dlt model
    $W_(upright("nef"))$ with nef transformed adjoint
  $ H_(upright("nef")) = K_(W_(upright("nef"))) + T_(upright("nef")) + M_(upright("nef")) . $
  Here $M_(upright("nef"))$ is the trace of the nef b-divisor whose nef representative is $M$ on $W$.
    On a common smooth resolution $mu : W' arrow.r W$, $sigma : W' arrow.r W_(upright("nef"))$, the actual comparison is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.9)")
   $ mu^(*) H tilde.op_(bb(Q)) sigma^(*) H_(upright("nef")) + E_(upright("nef")) \, wide E_(upright("nef")) gt.eq
     0 med sigma upright("-exceptional") . $ <rank:first-nef-endpoint>]
  The program extracts no divisors. We use this particular model also when the nef data are rationally trivial.
  If $kappa \( H \) > 0$, Equation (5.9) preserves the divisible section spaces.
    For $kappa \( H \) = k$ the nef $H_(upright("nef"))$ is big, and the second alternative holds with $W_m =
    W_(upright("nef"))$ and $H_m = H_(upright("nef"))$. If $0 < kappa \( H \) < k$,
    the exact global comparison in Lemma 78 gives $kappa \( J \) = kappa \( H \)$.
    Take the Iitaka fibration of $J$ on the source. Its projective image,
    followed by Stein factorization and a projective resolution, has dimension $kappa \( H \)$.
    The log adjoint on very general fibers has Kodaira dimension zero by the relative Iitaka construction
    #cite(label("CI")) Lemma 2.6, including its persistence under the reduced-exceptional source convention.
    This is the first alternative.
  Suppose $kappa \( H \) lt.eq 0$ and $M tilde.op_(bb(Q)) 0$.
    We may choose zero nef data in this rational equivalence class.
    The same generalized dlt model $W_(upright("nef"))$ is then an ordinary dlt model and $H_(upright("nef"))
    tilde.op_(bb(Q)) K_(W_(upright("nef"))) + T_(upright("nef"))$.
    Its adjoint is nef, so Proposition 12 makes $H_(upright("nef"))$ semiample as an actual rational line.
    Since $kappa \( H_(upright("nef")) \) = kappa \( H \) lt.eq 0$,
    a generated multiple has constant image and is trivial. Thus the second alternative holds with $W_m =
    W_(upright("nef"))$ and $H_m = H_(upright("nef")) tilde.op_(bb(Q)) 0$.
  It remains to suppose
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.10)")
   $ kappa \( H \) lt.eq 0 \, wide M ≁_(bb(Q)) 0 . $ <rank:nontrivial-nef-data>]
  Here $dim S > 0$. For a rational $c in \( 0 \, 1 \)$, put $H_c = K_W + T + c M$. We claim
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.11)")
   $ H_c med upright("is not pseudo-effective") quad \( 0 < c < 1 \, med c in bb(Q) \) . $ <rank:lowered-nonpsef>]
  Suppose otherwise for one $c$, and choose an ample rational line $U$ on $S$.
    We will find $delta > 0$ for which $H - delta p^(*) U$ has an effective representative,
    forcing $kappa \( H \) gt.eq dim S > 0$. Choose a rational $a > max { 1 \,
    a_0 }$ and a small rational $lambda > 0$ such that $K_S + a P_S - lambda U$ is big.
    This is possible because $P_S$ is nef and $K_S + a_0 P_S$ is big. Choose
  $ 0 < eta < lambda frac(1 - c, a - 1) quad upright("rational") . $
  The rational line $c P_S + eta U$ is ample. A sufficiently high general member divided by its degree pulls back
    to a rational boundary transverse to $T$ with subunit coefficient.
    Applying the nonvanishing part of Proposition 12 to this ordinary projective log pair gives
  $ E_c tilde.op_(bb(Q)) H_c + eta p^(*) U \, wide E_c gt.eq 0 . $
  A nonzero section defining $E_c$ restricts nontrivially to very general $p$-fibers in a fixed divisible degree.
    The hypotheses of the weak-effectivity statement #cite(label("CI")) Lemma 3.3 are now satisfied:
    the source is smooth in class $cal(C)$, $T$ is rational SNC, the base $S$ is smooth projective,
    and a relative log system is nonzero. Applied to the big line $K_S + a P_S - lambda U$, it gives
  $ E_a tilde.op_(bb(Q)) K_W + T + a M - lambda p^(*) U \, wide E_a gt.eq 0 . $
  For
  $ theta = frac(1 - c, a - c) \, wide delta = theta lambda - \( 1 - theta \) eta > 0 \, $
  the rational interpolation is
  $ \( 1 - theta \) E_c + theta E_a tilde.op_(bb(Q)) H - delta p^(*) U . $
  Multiplying pullbacks of sections of $U$ by a section of the effective left-hand side yields $kappa \( H \)
    gt.eq kappa \( p^(*) U \) = dim S > 0$. This contradicts Equation (5.10) and proves Equation (5.11).
  We next run a program to fiber type while descending the actual nef line $H_(upright("nef"))$ through every
    contraction. Choose the common resolution in Equation (5.9) to determine the nef data. The negativity lemma gives
  $ F_M := sigma^(*) M_(upright("nef")) - mu^(*) M gt.eq 0 quad sigma upright("-exceptional") . $
  Indeed its negative is $sigma$-nef and exceptional. For every rational $0 < e < 1$, Equation (5.9) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.12)")
   $ mu^(*) H_(1 - e) tilde.op_(bb(Q)) sigma^(*) \( H_(upright("nef")) - e M_(upright("nef")) \) +
     E_(upright("nef")) + e F_M . $ <rank:lowered-comparison>]
  If $H_(upright("nef")) - e M_(upright("nef"))$ were pseudo-effective,
    this equality and effectivity of the last two terms would make $mu^(*) H_(1 - e)$, and hence $H_(1 - e)$,
    pseudo-effective, contrary to Equation (5.11).
  Fix rational $0 < epsilon < 1$, choose an integer $r > 0$ with $r H_(upright("nef"))$ Cartier,
    and choose an integer $d_0 > 2 k r$. This coefficient will force every ray of the second program to have zero
    degree for the descended nef line. Decreasing the nef data to $\( 1 - epsilon \)
    bold(M)$ preserves generalized dlt singularities: on the displayed resolution the crepant boundary changes by
    $- epsilon F_M$, so discrepancies do not decrease. Add $d_0 H_(upright("nef"))$,
    determined as nef data on $W_(upright("nef"))$. It changes no discrepancies, and the resulting adjoint is
  $ \( 1 + d_0 \) H_(upright("nef")) - epsilon M_(upright("nef")) = \( 1 + d_0 \)
    (H_(upright("nef")) - frac(epsilon, 1 + d_0) M_(upright("nef"))) . $
  It is not pseudo-effective by the preceding paragraph. The non-pseudo-effective case of Proposition 81 therefore
    gives a chosen terminating program to a Mori fiber contraction in the $bb(Q)$-factorial generalized dlt category.
  At any stage of this second program, let $H_i$ be the descended rational line,
    assuming inductively that it is nef and $r H_i$ is Cartier, and write
  $ G_i = K_(W_i) + T_i + \( 1 - epsilon \) M_i . $
  An extremal ray negative for $G_i + d_0 H_i$ is negative for $G_i$.
    The pair with adjoint $G_i$ is still generalized dlt: the added nef b-divisor $d_0
    H_i$ descends to the current model, so removing it changes no discrepancy.
    The generalized length bound #cite(label("HanLi")) Proposition 3.13 gives a curve $C$ on that ray with $0 < -
    G_i dot.op C lt.eq 2 k$. If $H_i dot.op C > 0$, Cartier integrality would give
  $ d_0 / r lt.eq d_0 H_i dot.op C < - G_i dot.op C lt.eq 2 k \, $
  a contradiction. Thus $H_i dot.op C = 0$. The exact contraction statement #cite(label("Xie2022")) Theorem 1.5
    descends the Cartier line $r H_i$ along this contraction as an actual Cartier line.
    In a flip we pull that same line to the flipped side. The descended line is nef:
    every curve on the contraction base has a curve above it mapping with positive degree,
    so its degree is nonnegative; pullback preserves nefness.
    Consequently $r$ and nefness persist, and the argument applies inductively to all steps and to the final
    contraction. Each step is crepant for $H_i$.
  Let $W_m$ be the last birational model of this second program and let $H_m$ be the descended line on it.
    Crepancy for the $H_i$, together with the absence of extraction,
    turns Equation (5.9) into Equation (5.8) on a common resolution,
    with an effective divisor exceptional over $W_m$. The final contraction $h : W_m arrow.r
    V$ has connected fibers and, by the same exact descent,
  $ H_m tilde.op_(bb(Q)) h^(*) H_V $
  for a rational line $H_V$ on the normal projective $V$. If $V$ is a point,
    the second alternative holds with $H_m tilde.op_(bb(Q)) 0$.
  Assume $dim V > 0$. To obtain the first alternative, it remains to show that the log adjoint on the new very
    general fibers has Kodaira dimension zero. Resolve the base program by $mu : W'' arrow.r W$ and $nu :
    W'' arrow.r W_m$, and prepare the source over $W''$, calling it $g'' : \( X'' \, B'' \) arrow.r W''$.
    The diagram we use is
  #cetz.canvas({
  import cetz.draw: *
  content((0, 0), [$X''$])
  content((1.86, 0), [$W''$])
  content((3.719, 0), [$W_m$])
  content((5.447, 0), [$V$])
  content((1.86, -1.239), [$W$])
  line((0.468, 0), (1.392, 0), mark: (end: ">"))
  content((0.9298, 0.2266), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$g''$]])
  line((2.328, 0), (3.251, 0), mark: (end: ">"))
  content((2.789, 0.2266), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$nu$]])
  line((1.86, -0.2725), (1.86, -0.9661), mark: (end: ">"))
  content((1.585, -0.6193), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$mu$]])
  line((4.187, 0), (5.111, 0), mark: (end: ">"))
  content((4.649, 0.2266), [#text(size: 0.73em)[$h$]])
})
  with composite $f = h nu g''$. Equation (5.4) and Equation (5.8) give
  $ H'' tilde.op_(bb(Q)) nu^(*) H_m + E'' \, wide E'' gt.eq 0 med nu upright("-exceptional") . $
  To see the exceptionality, the error in Equation (5.8) is already exceptional over $W_m$,
    and every $mu$-exceptional prime is exceptional over $W_m$ as well:
    otherwise $W_m$ would extract a divisor over $W$. For every degree divisible by one fixed integer,
    Equation (5.3), exceptional descent, and projection formula now yield
  $ f_(*) cal(O)_(X'') \( q \( K_(X'') + B'' \) \) & arrow.r.hook \( h nu \)_(*) cal(O)_(W'') \( q H'' \)\
 & = h_(*) cal(O)_(W_m) \( q H_m \) = cal(O)_V \( q H_V \) . $
  Here $nu_(*) cal(O)_(W'') \( q E'' \) = cal(O)_(W_m)$ by normality and $h_(*) cal(O)_(W_m) = cal(O)_V$.
    Generic coherent base change, simultaneously for the countably many divisible degrees,
    bounds the dimension of every such system on a very general $f$-fiber by one.
    The log adjoint on that fiber is pseudo-effective by restriction of a countable sequence of perturbed currents
    with analytic singularities. Its dimension is less than $n$,
    so the lower-dimensional $cal(G)$ supplies a nonzero section in some divisible degree.
    Taking a common multiple shows that the fiber log Kodaira dimension is zero.
    All maps in the composite have connected fibers; resolving $V$ and the source keeps this property and gives a
    smooth projective base of dimension $0 < dim V < k$. This is the first alternative. ◻
]

=== 5.5 The negative divisor upstairs <the-negative-divisor-upstairs>

The next lemma lifts either base decomposition and determines the whole negative divisor on the source,
  including primes over subsets of codimension at least two in the base that the local comparison did not test.

#thm([Lemma 5.8.])[
  (Lifting the decomposition).  #emph[Assume the setting of Lemma 79.
    Suppose one of the following additional conditions holds:]
  + #emph[$a \( W \) = 0$ and $H tilde.op_(bb(Q)) E_W = N \( H \)$, with $E_W gt.eq 0$;]
  + #emph[there is a birational morphism $nu : W arrow.r W_m$ to a normal $bb(Q)$-factorial projective variety and
    an identity
$ H tilde.op_(bb(Q)) nu^(*) H_m + E_W \, wide E_W gt.eq 0 med nu upright("-exceptional") \, $

 where $H_m$ is a nef rational line, either big or rationally trivial.]
  #emph[Then there is an effective rational divisor $A$ on $X$ and an actual rational line identity
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.13)")
 $ J tilde.op_(bb(Q)) P_0 + A \, wide A = N \( J \) gt.eq 0 \, $ <rank:upstairs-decomposition>]

 where $P_0 tilde.op_(bb(Q)) 0$ in the first case and in the rationally trivial part of the second case,
   and $P_0 = \( nu g \)^(*) H_m$ in the big part of the second case.]
] <rank:lift>

#proof[
  Use the chosen base identity in Equation (5.1). In a rationally trivial case fix a rational trivialization of
    the positive line and put $P_0 = 0$; in the big case put $P_0 = \( nu g \)^(*) H_m$. Define the rational divisor
  $ A := A_(*) + g^(*) E_W . $
  The composed actual line identities give $J tilde.op_(bb(Q)) P_0 + A$.
    The divisor $A$ may at first have signed coefficients. We first prove $A gt.eq 0$.
  In a rationally trivial case a divisible multiple of the base line has a section whose normalized zero divisor
    is exactly $E_W$. Its section under the exact global comparison has normalized zero divisor $A$.
    Regularity of this section proves $A gt.eq 0$, also on the initially untested primes.
    In the big case choose a Kodaira decomposition
  $ H_m tilde.op_(bb(Q)) A_0 + G_0 \, wide A_0 upright(" ample") \, quad G_0 gt.eq 0 . $
  For rational $0 < t < 1$,
  $ H_m tilde.op_(bb(Q)) underbrace(\( 1 - t \) H_m + t A_0, upright("ample")) + t G_0 . $
  Fix a prime $Gamma subset X$. A sufficiently divisible section of the ample summand can be chosen not to vanish
    at the generic point of $\( nu g \) \( Gamma \)$; its pullback has zero order at $Gamma$.
    Multiplication by the section of $t G_0$ and then the global comparison gives a regular upstairs section.
    Its normalized order is
  $ A_Gamma + t thin "ord"_Gamma \( \( nu g \)^(*) G_0 \) gt.eq 0 . $
  Letting $t arrow.b 0$ proves $A_Gamma gt.eq 0$. This applies to every prime in $A$.
  Nefness of $P_0$ now implies $N \( J \) lt.eq A$. Set $Q = A - N \( J \) gt.eq 0$, an effective real divisor.
    By Equation (5.6), $Q$ is vertical. Moreover
  $ { P_0 + Q } = { J } - { N \( J \) } $
  is modified nef. We prove $Q = 0$.
  Let $b : X arrow.r Y$ denote $g : X arrow.r W$ in the first case and $nu g : X arrow.r W_m$ in the second.
    Thus $dim Y = k$, and $b$ has connected fibers. If $Q eq.not 0$, let
  $ l = max { dim b \( Gamma \) : Gamma upright(" a component of ") Q } lt.eq k - 1 . $
  Choose a Kähler class $eta$ on $Y$ (an ample class when $Y$ is projective) and a Kähler form $omega$ on $X$.
    On $H^(1 \, 1) \( X \, bb(R) \)$ define
  $ chevron.l alpha \, beta chevron.r_l = integral_X alpha beta \( b^(*) eta \)^l omega^(thin n - l - 2) . $
  In this pairing a divisor or rational line denotes its cohomology class.
    The exponent is nonnegative since $l lt.eq k - 1 lt.eq n - 2$.
    The restriction of a modified-nef class to a resolution of every prime is pseudo-effective.
    Applying this to ${ P_0 + Q }$ and pairing on each component of $Q$ gives $chevron.l P_0 + Q \,
    Gamma chevron.r_l gt.eq 0$. The $P_0$-term vanishes by dimension,
    since it is a pullback from $Y$ and $dim b \( Gamma \) lt.eq l$. Summing with the coefficients of $Q$ gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.14)")
   $ chevron.l Q \, Q chevron.r_l gt.eq 0 . $ <rank:nonnegative-square>]
  We now separate images of codimension at least two, where the mixed Hodge index theorem gives strict negativity,
    from divisorial images, where only a multiple of the whole fiber can have square zero.
  If $l < k - 1$, the mixed Hodge index theorem contradicts this inequality.
    In this form $b^(*) eta$ has positive square because $l + 2 lt.eq k$, while $chevron.l Q \,
    b^(*) eta chevron.r_l = 0$ by image dimension. The form is a limit of the mixed Hodge index forms obtained by
    replacing $b^(*) eta$ with $b^(*) eta + epsilon.alt \[ omega \]$;
    it therefore has at most one positive direction. In the orthogonal complement of a positive-square vector it
    is negative semidefinite, with equality only in the radical of the whole form.
    But $chevron.l Q \, omega chevron.r_l > 0$, since at least one component of $Q$ has image dimension $l$.
    Thus $Q$ is not in the radical, and its square is strictly negative, contrary to Equation (5.14).
  It remains to treat $l = k - 1$. Terms from primes whose image has smaller dimension vanish in this form,
    as do cross terms from different divisorial images. Fix a prime divisor $D subset
    Y$ and take all the primes $E_1 \, dots.h \, E_s subset X$ dominating it.
    Write $a_i = "ord"_(E_i) \( b^(*) D \) > 0$ and $C_(i j) = chevron.l E_i \, E_j chevron.r_(k - 1)$.
    The divisor $D$ is rational Cartier, also when $Y = W_m$. Consequently
  $ C_(i j) gt.eq 0 med \( i eq.not j \) \, wide sum_j C_(i j) a_j = 0 . $
  The first assertion is positivity of the proper intersection of distinct effective primes.
    The second pairs $E_i$ with the pulled-back divisor $D$; the additional base factor makes the intersection
    vanish by dimension. Components of $b^(*) D$ mapping into a proper subset of $D$ make no contribution.
    The graph with edges $C_(i j) > 0$ is connected. Indeed over a very general point of $D$ the fiber is
    connected, all its points lie in the components $E_i$, and an intersection contributes a positive entry
    precisely when it dominates $D$.
  For any real vector $x = \( x_i \)$, the two displayed properties give the exact identity
  $ x^(sans(T)) C x = - sum_(i < j) C_(i j) a_i a_j (x_i / a_i - x_j / a_j)^2 . $
  Thus the block is negative semidefinite with kernel spanned by $\( a_i \)$.
    Equation (5.14) forces every nonzero block of $Q$ to equal a positive scalar multiple of $\( a_i \)$.
    In particular $Q$ then contains every prime over that divisorial image $D$.
  In the second case this is impossible. The morphism $nu : W arrow.r
    W_m$ is an isomorphism over the generic point of $D$, and $E_W$ is exceptional.
    Equation (5.2) therefore supplies a component over $D$ whose $A$-coefficient is zero.
    Its $Q$-coefficient is zero as well, contradicting the preceding conclusion.
  In the first case, the same zero minimum shows that each divisorial image of a nonzero block of $Q$ is a
    component of $E_W = N \( H \)$. Here ${ Q }$ itself is modified nef. The class
  $ b_(*) \( { Q } \[ omega \]^(thin n - k) \) $
  is also modified nef on the smooth $W$. To see this directly,
    choose small-perturbation positive currents for ${ Q }$ with zero generic divisorial Lelong numbers,
    wedge them with $omega^(n - k)$, and push forward. Such a current has no trace mass on a divisor,
    so its push has no mass on a base divisor: the inverse image is a union of divisors and subsets of higher
    codimension, all of zero trace mass. The pushed currents therefore have zero generic divisorial Lelong
    numbers. Their classes tend to the displayed class; a fixed smooth representative for the vanishing error
    converts this into the defining small-Kähler-perturbation condition for modified nefness.
    On the other hand, pushing the actual divisor $Q$ in this formula kills the components with smaller image and
    gives a nonzero positive linear combination of the components of $N \( H \)$.
    Boucksom’s exceptionality of the components of a divisorial negative part says that no such combination is
    modified nef #cite(label("Boucksom2004")) Definition 3.10 and Theorem 3.12.
    This final contradiction proves $Q = 0$, and hence Equation (5.13). ◻
]

=== 5.6 Generation from a big nef line <generation-from-a-big-nef-line>

The remaining nef part is pulled back from a big nef line on a projective variety.
  We use generation along the log canonical boundary to prove that the adjoint itself is semiample.

#thm([Proposition 5.9.])[
  (Generation from a big nef base line).  #emph[Assume $cal(G)_d$ for $d < n$.
    Let $\( Z \, Delta_Z \)$ be a globally $bb(Q)$-factorial dlt compact Kähler pair of dimension $n$ satisfying
    the resolution condition of Definition 16, with effective rational boundary and analytically nef adjoint $L =
    K_Z + Delta_Z$. Suppose there are a smooth compact Kähler manifold $U$, a projective resolution $r : U arrow.r
    Z$, a proper surjection $f : U arrow.r Y$ to a normal projective variety,
    and a big nef rational line $H_Y$ on $Y$ such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.15)")
 $ r^(*) L tilde.op_(bb(Q)) f^(*) H_Y $ <rank:big-pullback>]

 as actual rational lines. Then $L$ is semiample.]
] <rank:big-nef-generation>

#proof[
  Put $D_Z = floor.l Delta_Z floor.r$. We first prove the following assertion for every pair and diagram
    satisfying the hypotheses of this proposition:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.16)")
   $ upright(bold(B)) \( L \) ∩ D_Z = diameter . $ <rank:floor-avoidance>]
  Here $upright(bold(B)) \( L \)$ is the intersection of the base loci of all positive Cartier multiples of $L$.
    Once this is proved, a log canonical threshold at any remaining stable base locus will create a new floor
    there and give a contradiction.
  Take a higher log resolution if needed and put
  $ K_U + F = r^(*) L = : ell \, wide C = F^(= 1) \, wide E = C - floor.l F floor.r \, wide Delta_F = F -
    floor.l F floor.r . $
  The equality defining $F$ uses the canonical meromorphic identification. The divisor $F$ is an SNC subboundary.
    The integral divisor $E$ is effective, $r$-exceptional, and has no component in common with $C$:
    a nonexceptional coefficient of $F$ is a coefficient of the effective $Delta_Z$,
    while every coefficient of $F$ is at most one. The image of $C$ is contained in $D_Z$.
    By Theorem 66, a divisible multiple of $L \|_(D_Z)$ is globally generated on the whole reduced floor.
  Suppose first that a component $C_i$ dominates $Y$. Pulling the floor sections to $C_i$ shows that
    $\( f \|_(C_i) \)^(*) H_Y tilde.op_(bb(Q)) ell \|_(C_i)$ is semiample.
    Semiampleness descends under a proper surjection to a normal space in this situation.
    Factor $C_i arrow.r Y$ through its normal Stein factor $Y_i$.
    Projection formula descends generation through the connected-fiber map.
    For the finite map $Y_i arrow.r Y$, at each $y in Y$ choose a section of the generated pullback line avoiding
    every point of the finite fiber; finitely many evaluation kernels cannot cover the section space.
    Lemma 10 gives a norm section of a fixed power of the line on $Y$, nonzero at $y$.
    These sections generate a fixed multiple of $H_Y$. Equation (5.15) and normal birational descent then give
    semiampleness of $L$.
  We may therefore assume that $C$ is vertical over $Y$. Choose a sufficiently divisible integer $u >
    1$ for which $u L \|_(D_Z)$ is generated, and consider the integral line
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.17)")
   $ N := cal(O)_U \( u ell + E - C \) = cal(O)_U #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] K_U +
     Delta_F + \( u - 1 \) ell #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <rank:vanishing-line>]
  Although the expression on the right uses rational divisors, its sum is the integral line on the left.
    The restriction sequence is
  $ 0 arrow.r  N arrow.r cal(O)_U \( u ell + E \) arrow.r cal(O)_C \( u ell + E \) arrow.r 0 . $
  The direct image of the last sheaf is supported on the proper analytic subset $f \( C \)$, so it is torsion.
    Consequently, the two assertions
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.18)")
   $ R^1 f_(*)  N med upright("torsion-free") \, wide H^1 \( Y \, f_(*)  N \) = 0 $ <rank:vanishing>]
  will give surjectivity on global sections: torsion-freeness makes the connecting map to $R^1 f_(*)  N$ zero,
    and the $H^1$-vanishing then removes the obstruction to lifting a global section.
  We prove Equation (5.18) using analytic injectivity. Equation (5.15) identifies $ N ⊗
    K_U^(- 1)$ with $Delta_F + \( u - 1 \) f^(*) H_Y$ in $"Pic" \( U \) ⊗ bb(Q)$;
    this comparison need not be an isomorphism of the corresponding integral lines.
    Fix a very ample line $A_Y$ on $Y$ and its Fubini–Study metric.
    A Kodaira decomposition of the big nef $H_Y$, given arbitrarily small weight as in the proof of Lemma 84,
    writes $\( u - 1 \) H_Y$ as a positive rational multiple of $A_Y$ plus an effective rational divisor whose
    fixed singular contribution is arbitrarily small and whose remaining contribution is a general divided ample
    member. Choose the small weight below the log canonical threshold of its pullback relative to the klt SNC
    boundary $Delta_F$, and choose the ample member generally. Then the resulting boundary on $U$ is klt.
    Choose a positive integer $q_0$ clearing all its denominators and realizing both the chosen Kodaira
    decomposition and the preceding rational-line comparison as line isomorphisms, in particular
  $ \(  N ⊗ K_U^(- 1) \)^(⊗ q_0) tilde.eq cal(O)_U \( q_0 Delta_F \) ⊗ f^(*) cal(O)_Y #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(]
    q_0 \( u - 1 \) H_Y #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  The divisor metrics and the Fubini–Study metric define a singular Hermitian metric on
    $\(  N ⊗ K_U^(- 1) \)^(⊗ q_0)$. Its $q_0$-th root is a metric on the actual line $ N ⊗ K_U^(- 1)$,
    with multiplier ideal $cal(O)_U$ and curvature dominating a positive multiple of the pullback Fubini–Study
    form. The same statements hold after every nonnegative twist by $f^(*) A_Y$.
  The injectivity theorem #cite(label("FujinoMatsumura")) Theorem A applies on the compact Kähler $U$:
    multiplication by the pullback of any nonzero section of a positive power of $A_Y$ is injective on
  $ H^1 \( U \,  N ⊗ f^(*) A_Y^j \) quad \( j gt.eq 0 \) $
  into the correspondingly higher twist. Its assumptions are exactly the semipositive smooth metric on the
    multiplying line, the preceding positive lower curvature bound, and the trivial multiplier ideal.
    This injectivity implies Equation (5.18). For the second assertion,
    the Leray edge map injects $H^1 \( Y \, f_(*)  N \)$ into $H^1 \( U \,  N \)$.
    Multiplication by a section of a sufficiently high $A_Y$-power sends this subspace to zero,
    since Serre vanishing kills $H^1 \( Y \, f_(*)  N ⊗ A_Y^j \)$ for large $j$.
    Injectivity upstairs forces the original subspace to be zero.
    For the first assertion, suppose $R^1 f_(*)  N$ has a nonzero torsion subsheaf.
    A section of a sufficiently high power of $A_Y$ annihilates a nonzero such subsheaf.
    After a further sufficiently large twist it has a nonzero global section,
    and Serre vanishing and Leray identify that section with a nonzero class in $H^1 \( U \,  N ⊗ f^(*) A_Y^j \)$.
    Multiplication annihilates this class, again contradicting injectivity. This proves both assertions.
  The resulting surjectivity extends the floor sections as follows.
    Pull any section of $u L \|_(D_Z)$ to $C$, multiply by the canonical section of $E \|_C$,
    and extend by this surjectivity. The extended section descends to $Z$, since $r_(*) cal(O)_U \( E \) =
    cal(O)_Z$ by exceptionality and normality. On the strict transforms of the components of $D_Z$,
    division by the canonical section of $E$ recovers the prescribed section.
    The descended restriction therefore equals it on the whole reduced $D_Z$,
    since equality holds generically on every component. Since $u L \|_(D_Z)$ is generated, this proves Equation (5.16).
  We finish by showing that this stable base locus is empty.
    The base loci of factorial Cartier multiples form a descending sequence of compact analytic subsets and hence
    stabilize. Sections exist because $H_Y$ is big and Equation (5.15) descends their pullbacks.
    Choose a divisible degree $v$ whose base locus is $upright(bold(B)) \( L \)$,
    and let $frak(b)$ be its base ideal. If $upright(bold(B)) \( L \) eq.not diameter$,
    the ideal is a unit near $D_Z$, while $\( Z \, Delta_Z \)$ is klt outside $D_Z$. Its log canonical threshold
  $ t = "lct"_(\( Z \, Delta_Z \)) \( frak(b) \) $
  is therefore a positive rational number, attained by a divisor whose center is contained in
    $upright(bold(B)) \( L \)$. This follows directly on a simultaneous log resolution of the pair and the ideal:
    the threshold is the minimum of the positive rational discrepancies divided by the positive integral ideal orders.
  Choose an integer $s > t$ and general divisors $D_1 \, dots.h \, D_s in \| v L \|$.
    On the same resolution their fixed part is the divisor of $frak(b)$;
    their free transforms are jointly transverse to the SNC data. Hence
  $ (Z \, med Delta_Z + t / s sum_(i = 1)^s D_i) $
  is log canonical: the fixed part is at its log canonical threshold and the free components have coefficients
    $t \/ s < 1$. There is a new lc place centered in $upright(bold(B)) \( L \)$,
    and the new adjoint satisfies the actual identity
  $ L_(upright("new")) tilde.op_(bb(Q)) \( 1 + t v \) L . $
  Apply #cite(label("HLX")) Theorem 3.3 to this compact Kähler lc rational pair.
    It gives a projective strongly $bb(Q)$-factorial dlt modification $d : Z' arrow.r Z$.
    Since the pair is lc, all extracted divisors have log discrepancy zero in our convention,
    so the modification is crepant. The source is again compact Kähler.
    Choose its defining dlt log resolution, with exceptional crepant coefficients strictly below one and an
    isomorphism at the general point of every lc stratum; resolving the remaining data away from those points
    gives the resolution required by Definition 16. The floor on $Z'$ has a point over the center of this lc
    place, hence over $upright(bold(B)) \( L \)$. The new nef adjoint is $d^(*) L_(upright("new"))$.
    On a common resolution with $U$, it satisfies Equation (5.15) with the big nef line $\( 1 + t v \) H_Y$.
    The previously proved assertion (5.16) therefore makes its stable base locus disjoint from its floor.
    On the other hand, normality and projection formula identify all divisible sections under $d$, and give
  $ upright(bold(B)) \( d^(*) L_(upright("new")) \) = d^(- 1) upright(bold(B)) \( L \) . $
  This locus contains the stated floor point, a contradiction.
    Thus $upright(bold(B)) \( L \) = diameter$; factorial stabilization provides an actual globally generated
    multiple of $L$. ◻
]

=== 5.7 Completion of the rank-one case <completion-of-the-rank-one-case>

#proof[
  By Proposition 9 we can begin on the resolved source in the statement.
