The next lemma turns the pole bound in Equation (6.27) into a common vanishing order for the coefficients of each
  determinant map.

#thm([Lemma 6.10.])[
  #emph[On a smooth projective modification $h_(+) : U^(+) arrow.r E^(+)$ one can choose an approximation
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.31)")
 $ h_(+)^(*) \( 2 p_(+)^(*) M + s zeta \) = beta^(+) + { D^(+) } \, wide beta^(+) med upright("Kähler") \, quad
   D^(+) gt.eq 0 med upright("rational") \, $ <nv:plus-decomposition>]

 retaining the log-ideal orders of $T \|_E$, such that the general fiber volume $w^(+)$ of $beta^(+)$ over $Z^(+)$
   satisfies $w^(+) gt.eq w \/ 2$. Let $f_(+) : U^(+) arrow.r Z^(+)$ be the natural map and put
$ Lambda^(+) = s h_(+)^(*) cal(O)_(E^(+)) \( 1 \) - cal(O)_(U^(+)) \( D^(+) \) \, wide
  cal(F)_j^(+) = \( f_(+) \)_(*) cal(O)_(U^(+)) \( j Lambda^(+) \) \, wide R_j^(+) = "rk" cal(F)_j^(+) . $

 For every sufficiently large divisible $j$, write
$ iota_j : cal(F)_j^(+) arrow.r.hook p_(+)^(*) "Sym"^(j s) Omega_Z \, wide phi_j : det cal(F)_j^(+) arrow.r
  and.big^(R_j^(+)) #h(-0.16667em) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] p_(+)^(*) "Sym"^(j s) Omega_Z
  #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] $

 for the inclusion and its nonzero determinant map. Here $det
   cal(F)_j^(+) = \( and.big^(R_j^(+)) cal(F)_j^(+) \)^(* *)$,
   and the determinant map extends across the complement of the locally free locus,
   which has codimension at least two. Localize a coordinate local ring of $Z^(+)$ at the height-one prime of
   $J_(+)$, obtaining a discrete valuation ring $R$ with uniformizer $u$. In local frames over $R$, define
$ h_j = min { "ord"_u \( c \) : c upright(" is a nonzero coefficient of ") phi_j } . $

 Equivalently, $h_j$ is the minimum order of the maximal minors of $iota_j$; it is independent of the frames.
   If $sigma_(J_(+))$ is the canonical section of $cal(O)_(Z^(+)) \( J_(+) \)$, then $phi_j$ factors as
$ det cal(F)_j^(+) arrow.r^(med dot.op sigma_(J_(+))^(h_j) med) det cal(F)_j^(+) \( h_j J_(+) \)
  arrow.r^(med tilde(phi)_j med) and.big^(R_j^(+)) #h(-0.16667em) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] p_(+)^(*)
  "Sym"^(j s) Omega_Z #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, $

 where $tilde(phi)_j$ is holomorphic. The common order satisfies
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.32)")
 $ h_j gt.eq c_n thin j R_j^(+) s \, $ <nv:determinant-order-equation>]

 provided $q$ is sufficiently large.]
] <nv:determinant-order>

#proof[
  Blow up $cal(J)$ and take a common smooth projective resolution with $U arrow.r E$ from Equation (6.19).
    Pull back $beta$ and $D'$. Subtracting a sufficiently small rational multiple of an effective exceptional
    divisor whose negative is relatively ample makes the pulled-back Kähler class Kähler on this resolution;
    add that multiple to the effective part. Further upward rational rounding may be arbitrarily small.
    This gives Equation (6.31) with all original orders retained.
    Its general fiber intersection is as close to $w$ as desired, so arrange $w^(+) gt.eq w \/ 2$.
    Lemma 91 and the effective divisor give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.33)")
   $ cal(F)_j^(+) subset.eq p_(+)^(*) "Sym"^(j s) Omega_Z \, wide R_j^(+) = frac(w^(+), \( d - 1 \) !) j^(d - 1) +
     O \( j^(d - 2) \) . $ <nv:plus-rank>]
  We spell out the local order imposed on the polynomials in this inclusion.
    Near $z = \( x \, x \, lambda \)$, use coordinates $\( upright(bold(x)) \, upright(bold(a)) \, lambda \)$ on
    the first $Z$, where $upright(bold(x))$ and $upright(bold(a))$ each have $n$ components and
    $U_0 = \( upright(bold(a)) = 0 \)$. Write the second coordinates as
    $\( upright(bold(x)) + upright(bold(h)) \, upright(bold(a)) + upright(bold(k)) \, lambda + mu \)$,
    with $upright(bold(h)) \, upright(bold(k))$ each having $n$ components. Then
  $ cal(V)_0 = \( upright(bold(a)) = upright(bold(k)) = mu = 0 \) \, wide
    Delta_Z = \( upright(bold(h)) = upright(bold(k)) = mu = 0 \) . $
  In a blowup chart meeting the lines tangent to $D_lambda$, take
  $ h_1 = v \, quad h_i = v alpha_i med \( 2 lt.eq i lt.eq n \) \, quad k_i = v y_i med \( 1 lt.eq i lt.eq n \) \,
    quad mu = v y_(n + 1) . $
  Here $E = \( v = 0 \)$,
    while the strict transform is $V = \( a_1 = dots.h.c = a_n = y_1 = dots.h.c = y_(n + 1) = 0 \)$. In particular,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.34)")
   $ cal(I)_V \|_E = \( a_1 \, dots.h \, a_n \, y_1 \, dots.h \, y_(n + 1) \) . $ <nv:ideal-before>]
  On a chart of the blowup of $U_0$, write $a_1 = u$ and $a_i = u tau_i$ for $i gt.eq 2$.
    Along the general point of $J_(+) = \( u = 0 \)$, the ideal $cal(J)$ is exactly
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.35)")
   $ \( u \, y_1 \, dots.h \, y_(n + 1) \) . $ <nv:joint-ideal>]
  Here $u$ measures vanishing along $J_(+)$, while the $y_i$ measure transverse fiber directions.
    Thus a term of transverse degree $ell$ can contribute at most $ell$ to the incidence order;
    the remaining order must come from its coefficient in $u$. We now make this statement precise.
  Suppose locally that the singularities of $T$ are $c log \| frak(a) \| + O \( 1 \)$,
    with the logarithmic normalization for which a divisorial coefficient is $c$ times the ideal order.
    If $k = "ord"_V \( frak(a) \)$, then $b_V = c k$. The normal Taylor coefficients of degree less than $k$
    vanish on a dense open set of $V$, hence on $V$ locally. Thus $frak(a) subset.eq
    cal(I)_V^k$ also near a general point of $V ∩ E$. Restricting to $E$ and pulling to $E^(+)$ gives the
    corresponding order in Equation (6.35). At its general center the displayed generators are regular
    coordinates. If $H_(cal(J))$ is the exceptional divisor of their blowup,
    its valuation is the ordinary ideal-adic order:
  $ "ord"_(H_(cal(J))) \( g \) = max { k : g in \( u \, y_1 \, dots.h \, y_(n + 1) \)^k } . $
  The common resolution dominates this blowup. Pullback preserves the effective difference between $D'$ and the
    resolved log divisor, and the later adjustments only increase the effective part.
    Thus $D^(+)$ has coefficient at least $b_V$ at this valuation.
    Every polynomial image of a section of $cal(F)_j^(+)$ has integral valuation at least $ceil.l j b_V ceil.r$.
    Locally bounded remainders have zero order at this valuation.
  There are $n$ homogeneous coordinates along $T_z D_lambda$ and $n +
    1$ transverse homogeneous coordinates in $T_z Z$. Denote these two groups by $H_1 \, dots.h \,
    H_n$ and $Y_1 \, dots.h \, Y_(n + 1)$. For $m = j s$, a local polynomial in $"Sym"^m \( p_(+)^(*) Omega_Z \)$
    has the form
  $ sum_(\| alpha \| + \| gamma \| = m) c_(alpha \, gamma) \( u \) thin H^alpha Y^gamma . $
  Work over the discrete valuation ring $R$ used to define $h_j$, choosing the chart parameter $u$ as uniformizer,
    and let $kappa$ be its residue field. The coefficients $c_(alpha \, gamma)$ lie in $R$.
    Equation (6.35) implies, monomial by monomial,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.36)")
   $ "ord"_u \( c_(alpha \, gamma) \) gt.eq max { 0 \, ceil.l j b_V ceil.r - \| gamma \| } . $ <nv:coefficient-order>]
  Indeed, on the chart $H_1 eq.not 0$, group the dehomogenized polynomial as
  $ sum_gamma P_gamma \( H_2 \/ H_1 \, dots.h \, H_n \/ H_1 \) \( Y \/ H_1 \)^gamma \, wide
    P_gamma in R \[ H_2 \/ H_1 \, dots.h \, H_n \/ H_1 \] . $
  Put $b_j = ceil.l j b_V ceil.r$. At the general center of $\( u \, Y \/ H_1 \)$,
    order at least $b_j$ forces $P_gamma$ to be divisible by $u^(b_j - \| gamma \|)$ whenever $\| gamma \| < b_j$.
    Otherwise its first nonzero reduction would be a nonzero polynomial over $kappa$,
    which stays nonzero in $kappa \( H_2 \/ H_1 \, dots.h \, H_n \/ H_1 \)$;
    in the associated graded ring it would give a nonzero term of total $\( u \, Y \/ H_1 \)$-degree less than
    $b_j$. The transverse monomials there are independent. Comparing the internal polynomial coefficients over the
    base residue field $kappa$ now gives Equation (6.36).
  We compare the number of monomials with the rank estimate in Equation (6.33).
    The ambient rank and the number of monomials of transverse degree $ell$ are
  $ N_m = binom(m + 2 n, 2 n) \, wide N_(m \, ell) = binom(ell + n, n) binom(m - ell + n - 1, n - 1) . $
  For fixed $delta in \( 0 \, 1 \)$, let $H_m \( delta \)$ count the monomials with $ell > \( 1 - delta \) m$.
    Summing instead over their internal degree gives
  $ H_m \( delta \) lt.eq binom(m + n, n) binom(ceil.l delta m ceil.r + n, n) \, wide
    limsup_(m arrow.r oo) frac(H_m \( delta \), N_m) lt.eq binom(2 n, n) delta^n . $
  On the other hand, $w^(+) gt.eq w \/ 2$ and Equation (6.33) show that the approximation on $E^(+)$ retains at
    least half the rank fraction in Equation (6.26):
  $ lim_(j arrow.r oo) R_j^(+) / N_(j s) = w^(+) / s^(d - 1) gt.eq frac(w, 2 s^(d - 1)) gt.eq c_n > 0 . $
  Fix $delta > 0$, depending only on $n$, so small that, for each fixed $q$ and its selected data,
    fewer than $R_j^(+) \/ 2$ monomials have transverse degree greater than $\( 1 - delta \) j
    s$ for all sufficiently large divisible $j$. For each remaining monomial, Equation (6.36) and Lemma 94 give
  $ "ord"_u \( c_(alpha \, gamma) \) gt.eq j \( s - C_n \) - \( 1 - delta \) j s = j \( delta s - C_n \) gt.eq
    frac(delta j s, 2) . $
  The last inequality holds once $q$ is large, since $s gt.eq c_n A_q arrow.r oo$.
  Over the same discrete valuation ring $R$, the torsion-free sheaf $cal(F)_j^(+)$ becomes a free module of rank
    $R_j^(+)$. Write its inclusion into the symmetric power as a matrix with the monomials as rows.
    Every maximal minor uses at least $R_j^(+) \/ 2$ of the rows whose coefficients have order at least $delta j
    s \/ 2$. All maximal minors therefore have order at least $delta j R_j^(+) s \/ 4$.
    By the definition of $h_j$, this proves Equation (6.32). Dividing the determinant map by $sigma_(J_(+))^(h_j)$
    gives a holomorphic map at every point of codimension one:
    the coefficients have the required order along $J_(+)$, and its local equation is a unit away from $J_(+)$.
    Hartogs’ theorem extends the divided map across the remaining set of codimension at least two,
    giving the stated factorization through $det cal(F)_j^(+) \( h_j J_(+) \)$. ◻
]

#proof[
  #emph[Completion of the proof of Theorem 86.] Fix $q$ sufficiently large for the preceding lemmas,
    with its chosen $P \, s \, T$ and modifications. Choose a very general point $x$ in the first factor of $X^2$.
    The slice of $Z^(+)$ above $x$ is
  $ S = bb(P)_Y \( cal(O)_Y xor a^(*)  L \) \, wide a : Y = "Bl"_x X arrow.r X \, $
  and $J_(+) \|_S$ is the pullback of $F$. We choose $x$ so that Equation (6.2), Equation (6.8),
    the restrictions of the currents below, and all determinant maps for divisible $j$ can be tested on this
    slice. These exclude at most countably many proper analytic sets and the measure-zero exceptional sets for
    current restrictions.
  Write the restricted determinant line as
  $ \( det cal(F)_j^(+) \) \|_S = pi_S^(*)  Q_j^(+) ⊗ cal(O)_S \( ell_j^(+) \) \, wide Q_j^(+) = c_1 \(  Q_j^(+) \) . $
  Factoring the common zero of order $h_j$ from the determinant map gives a nonzero map whose source on $S$ is
  $ pi_S^(*) \(  Q_j^(+) ⊗ cal(O)_Y \( h_j F \) \) ⊗ cal(O)_S \( ell_j^(+) \) . $
  The plus sign of $h_j F$ follows because division of the map by the local equation of $J_(+)^(h_j)$ enlarges its
    source from $det cal(F)_j^(+)$ to $det cal(F)_j^(+) ⊗ cal(O) \( h_j J_(+) \)$.
    Its target embeds into the $j s R_j^(+)$-fold tensor of the restriction of $p_(+)^(*) Omega_Z$.
    In particular we use the cotangent bundle of the original $Z$, with its restricted filtration
  $ 0 arrow.r pi_S^(*) \( cal(O)_Y^(xor n) xor a^(*) Omega_X \) arrow.r \( p_(+)^(*) Omega_Z \) \|_S arrow.r
    cal(O)_S \( - 2 \) ⊗ pi_S^(*) a^(*)  L arrow.r 0 . $
  The same homogeneous-monomial calculation as in Equation (6.23),
    now applying Equation (6.2) to the second factor of $X^2$, gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.37)")
   $ ell_j^(+) lt.eq 0 \, wide Q_j^(+) + h_j { F } lt.eq \( j s R_j^(+) - ell_j^(+) \) a^(*) L .
     $ <nv:determinant-bound-slice>]
  More explicitly, if the chosen graded term has $i$ relative factors, the pushed polynomial has degree $- 2 i -
    ell_j^(+) gt.eq 0$. Its exponent in $a^(*)  L$, together with the $i$ relative factors and the cotangent order
    from the second factor of $X^2$, is at most $j s R_j^(+) - ell_j^(+)$.
    The cotangent factors from the first factor of $X^2$ are constant.
    This proves the displayed inequality using the tensors of $a^(*) Omega_X$,
    without introducing $Omega_Y$ or $Omega_(Z^(+))$.
  Apply Lemma 91 to the decomposition in Equation (6.31). Equation (6.14) shows that the limits of the restricted
    determinant components divided by $j R_j^(+)$ exist; denote them by $Q^(+)$ and $ell^(+)$.
    Divide Equation (6.37) by $j R_j^(+)$, use Equation (6.32), and pass to the closed pseudo-effective cone. We obtain
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.38)")
   $ ell^(+) lt.eq 0 \, wide Q^(+) + c_n s { F } lt.eq \( s - ell^(+) \) a^(*) L . $ <nv:slice-upper>]
  The pseudo-effective class in Equation (6.14), restricted to the very general slice $S$, is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.39)")
   $ pi_S^(*) \( Q^(+) + 2 a^(*) P \) + \( ell^(+) + 2 q \) xi gt.eq 0 . $ <nv:slice-psef>]
  Testing on a general vertical line gives $ell^(+) + 2 q gt.eq 0$.
    Let $A subset S$ be the axis of $bb(P)_Y \( cal(O)_Y xor a^(*)  L \)$ with divisor class ${ A } = xi$.
    On $A tilde.eq Y$, its normal class is ${ A } \|_A = xi \|_A = a^(*) L gt.eq 0$.
    Add an arbitrarily small Kähler class to the class in Equation (6.39),
    and choose a Kähler current with analytic singularities in the resulting big class.
    Remove its generic divisorial pole along $A$ and restrict the residual current to $A$.
    Adding back the removed nonnegative multiple of $a^(*) L$ preserves pseudo-effectivity. Passing to the limit gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.40)")
   $ Q^(+) + 2 a^(*) P + \( ell^(+) + 2 q \) a^(*) L gt.eq 0 . $ <nv:slice-lower>]
  The slope bound in Equation (6.38) supplies the pseudo-effective class $\( s - ell^(+) \) a^(*) L - Q^(+) -
    c_n s { F }$. Adding it to Equation (6.40) cancels the entire normalized determinant class restricted to the
    axis, $Q^(+) + ell^(+) a^(*) L$, leaving
  $ 2 a^(*) P + \( s + 2 q \) a^(*) L - c_n s { F } gt.eq 0 . $
  Since $L lt.eq P \/ r$, the point bound in Equation (6.8) implies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.41)")
   $ frac(c_n s, thin 2 + \( s + 2 q \) \/ r thin) lt.eq C_n . $ <nv:contradiction>]
  The denominator is bounded independently of $q$, because $s lt.eq C_n q^(1 \/ d)$ and $r gt.eq q^2$.
    The numerator tends to infinity, because $s gt.eq c_n q^(1 \/ d)$.
    Equation (6.41) is impossible for arbitrarily large $q$.
  For each $q$, the choice of $t \( q \)$, the rational $s$, the current $T$,
    and the modifications precedes the limit in $j$. The monomial cutoff $delta$ depends only on $n$,
    while the required lower bound for divisible $j$ may depend on the fixed data.
    We choose the very general points after those data are fixed.
    Thus every simultaneous general-point test above involves at most countably many conditions,
    and no bound depends on the point. The contradiction disproves the contrary hypothesis and proves Theorem 86. ◻
]

== 7 Signed rigidity on simple spaces <sec:signed>

The meromorphic section furnished by Theorem 86 need not have an effective divisor.
  We therefore need a boundary argument that retains both its zeroes and its poles.
  The result below supplies that argument.

#thm([Theorem 7.1.])[
  (Signed rigidity).  #emph[Let $\( X \, D \)$ be a dlt pair on a normal irreducible compact Kähler space,
    and suppose that the pair has the resolution property of Definition 16.
    Suppose also that $X$ is globally $bb(Q)$-factorial, that $a \( X \) = 0$, that $X$ is simple, and that $D =
    sum_(i = 1)^s D_i$ is reduced. Put $L = K_X + D$, and assume the following.]
  + #emph[The rational line $L$ is analytically nef and has an actual rational linear equivalence
$ L tilde.op_(bb(Q)) G = sum_(i = 1)^s a_i D_i \, wide a_i in bb(Q) . $

]
  + #emph[For some real $c > 0$, the pullback of the class ${ L -
    c D }$ to a projective resolution with smooth compact Kähler source is pseudo-effective.]
  + #emph[The rational holomorphic line $L \|_D$ is semiample on the whole reduced analytic space $D$:
    some Cartier multiple is generated at every point of $D$.]
  #emph[Then $L$ is torsion. More precisely, for some positive integer $q$,
    the holomorphic line $cal(O)_X \( q L \)$ is isomorphic to $cal(O)_X$.]
] <signed:torsion>

To apply this theorem in the proof of Proposition 14, Theorem 86 supplies a signed representative of the canonical
  bundle. After resolving, we enlarge the support of the transformed boundary and this signed canonical divisor to
  a reduced SNC divisor; the resulting adjoint remains pseudo-effective.
  Corollary 29 gives an ordinary dlt nef model $\( X_(upright("nef")) \, D_(upright("nef")) \)$ whose adjoint
  $L_(upright("nef")) = K_(X_(upright("nef"))) + D_(upright("nef"))$ has a signed representative supported on
  $D_(upright("nef"))$, supplying the first hypothesis. Theorem 66 supplies semiampleness on the whole reduced
  $D_(upright("nef"))$. On a common smooth resolution, simplicity excludes uniruledness,
  so Ou’s criterion #cite(label("Ou2025")) Theorem 1.1 and exceptional translation for the canonical comparison
  give pseudo-effectivity of the pullback of $K_(X_(upright("nef"))) = L_(upright("nef")) - D_(upright("nef"))$.
  Thus the second hypothesis holds with $c = 1$. Once signed rigidity makes the actual line $L_(upright("nef"))$
  torsion, the program comparison and Lemma 8 let us subtract the added boundary and give the required
  decomposition for the original adjoint. The final subsection verifies these steps.

The signed construction in #cite(label("LI")) Proposition 3.3 is the source of the root and separation operations
  used below. The lifting method is that of #cite(label("BL")) Sections 10–12.
  We will establish the analytic form of the signed construction and the change to the lifting argument caused by
  its residual poles. The numerical argument first singles out positive-dimensional fibers in the positive
  boundary. We then lift those fibers through every finite boundary neighborhood and deform them to compact
  subspaces outside the boundary. An argument using the cycle space and Baire’s theorem turns these deformations
  into a covering family, which simplicity excludes.

=== 7.1 The boundary detected by the nef class <the-boundary-detected-by-the-nef-class>

If $D = 0$, the signed equivalence already gives $L tilde.op_(bb(Q)) 0$.
  We may therefore assume in the constructions below that $D eq.not 0$; in particular $dim X > 0$.

Fix the data of Theorem 96, and write

$ G = G_(+) - G_(-) \, wide D_0 = sum_(a_i = 0) D_i \, $

where $G_(+)$ and $G_(-)$ are effective with disjoint prime supports.
  Choose a positive integer $m$ so that $m G_(+)$, $m G_(-)$, $m D_0$, and $m L$ are Cartier,
  the given rational equivalence induces a fixed isomorphism

$ N_0 := cal(O)_X \( m G \) tilde.eq cal(O)_X \( m L \) \, $

and $N_0 \|_D$ is generated. Its sections give a morphism and an actual line isomorphism

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.1)")
 $ phi.alt : D arrow.r P = bb(P)^b \, wide N_0 \|_D tilde.eq phi.alt^(*) cal(O)_P \( 1 \) . $ <signed:boundary-map>]

Let $n = dim X$. Choose a projective resolution $mu : tilde(X) arrow.r
  X$ as in the second hypothesis of the theorem, and a Kähler class $omega$ on $tilde(X)$.
  The numerical dimension of the nef pullback is

$ v = nu \( L \) := max { k in { 0 \, dots.h \, n } : \( mu^(*) { L } \)^k omega^(n - k) > 0
  #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[}] . $

The next argument is the Kähler form of #cite(label("LI")) Lemma 3.2.

#thm([Lemma 7.2.])[
  #emph[If $v = 0$, then $D = 0$ and $L tilde.op_(bb(Q)) 0$. If $v = n > 0$, then $a \( X \) = n$.
    If $0 < v < n$ and $r = v - 1$, then
$ dim phi.alt \( D_i \) lt.eq r quad upright("for every ") i \, wide max_(a_i > 0) dim phi.alt \( D_i \) = r \, $

 and
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.2)")
 $ dim phi.alt #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "Supp" G_(+) ∩ \( "Supp" G_(-) union D_0 \)
   #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] < r . $ <signed:small-intersections>]

 For $r = 0$, the intersection in Equation (7.2) is empty.]
] <signed:boundary-geometry>

#proof[
  Choose a Kähler class $theta$ on $X$. We use Cartier intersection products downstairs,
    computed on a resolution by projection formula. These products detect $v$.
    Indeed, on the chosen resolution the class $h = mu^(*) theta$ is nef and big.
    Choose $C \, epsilon.alt > 0$ such that $C omega - h$ is nef and $h - epsilon.alt omega$ is pseudo-effective.
    Pairing these two inequalities successively with products of the nef classes $mu^(*) { L }$, $h$,
    and $omega$ shows that
  $ \( mu^(*) { L } \)^k h^(n - k) > 0 quad arrow.l.r.double quad \( mu^(*) { L } \)^k omega^(n - k) > 0 . $
  We may therefore use $theta$ in all the tests defining $v$.
  Suppose first that $v < n$. The pseudo-effective pullback in the theorem can be paired with nef products, so
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.3)")
   $ 0 lt.eq \( { L } - c { D } \) { L }^v theta^(n - v - 1) = -
     c sum_i { D_i } { L }^v theta^(n - v - 1) . $ <signed:boundary-zero>]
  Each summand on the right is nonnegative. For example, pull the generated line in Equation (7.1) and $theta$ to
    a resolution of $D_i$; their representatives are semipositive and positive at general smooth points,
    respectively. Thus every summand is zero. When $v = 0$, a nonzero effective divisor has strictly positive
    $theta^(n - 1)$-degree. Hence $D = 0$, and the signed equivalence gives $L tilde.op_(bb(Q)) 0$.
  Now let $0 < v < n$. For any irreducible effective cycle $V$ in $D$,
    the mixed degree ${ L }^k theta^(dim V - k) dot.op V$ is positive exactly when $dim phi.alt \( V \) gt.eq k$.
    To see this, resolve $V$ and use a Fubini–Study representative for $m { L } \|_V$.
    Its generic rank is $dim phi.alt \( V \)$, and its wedge with the remaining Kähler factors is positive on a
    nonempty open set exactly in the asserted range. The vanishing in Equation (7.3) therefore gives $dim
    phi.alt \( D_i \) lt.eq r$. On the other hand,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.4)")
   $ 0 < { L }^v theta^(n - v) = sum_i a_i { D_i } { L }^r theta^(n - v) . $ <signed:positive-attainment>]
  All the component tests here are nonnegative, so a component with $a_i > 0$ attains dimension $r$.
  It remains to separate its intersections from a general fiber.
    On $H_(upright("BC"))^(1 \, 1) \( tilde(X) \, bb(R) \)$ consider the form
  $ q \( alpha \, beta \) = alpha beta \( mu^(*) { L } \)^r h^(n - r - 2) \, wide Q_(i j) =
    q \( mu^(*) { D_i } \, mu^(*) { D_j } \) . $
  The mixed Kähler Hodge index theorem, followed by approximation of the nef factors by Kähler classes,
    says that $q$ has at most one positive direction; see #cite(label("DinhNguyen")) Theorems A and C.
    Put $l = mu^(*) { L }$. The definition of $v$ and Equation (7.3) give
  $ q \( l \, l \) = 0 \, wide q \( l \, mu^(*) { D_i } \) = 0 \, wide q \( l \, h \) = { L }^v theta^(n - v) > 0 . $
  Linear algebra now makes $q$ negative semidefinite on $l^perp$:
    a positive vector in $l^perp$, together with a suitable vector in the span of $l \, h$,
    would give two positive directions. In particular $Q$ is negative semidefinite.
  For $i eq.not j$, one has $Q_(i j) gt.eq 0$. This assertion uses the effective intersection cycle of the
    distinct $bb(Q)$-Cartier divisors $D_i \, D_j$ on $X$, and projection formula.
    It does not require their total transforms to intersect effectively.
    The two Cartier equations have a proper intersection: the dlt ambient space is klt, hence Cohen–Macaulay,
    after dropping the reduced boundary, and the distinct divisor equations form a regular sequence.
    The intersection cycle is consequently pure of codimension two, and each of its mixed nef degrees is nonnegative.
  Write the coefficient vector as $upright(bold(a)) = upright(bold(a))^(+) - upright(bold(a))^(-)$,
    with nonnegative vectors of disjoint supports. The signed equivalence and orthogonality to $l$ give $Q
    upright(bold(a)) = 0$. Thus
  $ Q \( upright(bold(a))^(+) \, upright(bold(a))^(+) \) = Q \( upright(bold(a))^(+) \, upright(bold(a))^(-) \)
    gt.eq 0 . $
  Negative semidefiniteness forces equality and $Q upright(bold(a))^(+) = 0$.
    For every $j$ with $a_j lt.eq 0$, its row is a sum of nonnegative off-diagonal terms:
  $ 0 = sum_(a_i > 0) a_i Q_(j i) . $
  Each term is zero. Applying the generated-line degree test to the effective intersection cycles proves
    Equation (7.2). If $r = 0$, any nonempty intersection would have positive $theta^(n - 2)$-degree,
    so these intersections are empty.
  Finally, if $v = n > 0$, the nef volume criterion makes the rational line on $tilde(X)$ big
    #cite(label("DemaillyPaun2004")) Theorem 0.5. A big holomorphic line on a compact Kähler manifold has maximal
    Iitaka dimension. Hence $a \( tilde(X) \) = n$, and birational invariance gives $a \( X \) = n$. ◻
]

Thus, when $0 < v < n$, a positive component attaining image dimension $r = v -
  1$ has general fibers of dimension $\( n - 1 \) - r = n - v > 0$.
  Equation (7.2) makes those fibers disjoint from $"Supp" G_(-) union D_0$.
  The next construction prepares their normal directions and adjunction for infinitesimal lifting.

=== 7.2 Local roots and residue with residual poles <local-roots-and-residue-with-residual-poles>

We work for the rest of the signed argument in the range $0 < v < n$, with $r = v - 1$.
  Choose $ell > 1$ divisible by $m$ and by every nonzero integer $\| m a_i \|$, and put $a = ell \/ m$,
  a positive integer.

#thm([Lemma 7.3.])[
  (Analytic signed charts).  #emph[For each $t in P$, after shrinking an open neighborhood $U subset P$,
    choose a line $R_U$ together with an isomorphism $R_U^ell tilde.eq cal(O)_P \( 1 \) \|_U$.
    A comparison of two such roots on an overlap is power-compatible if its $ell$-th power commutes with these
    isomorphisms. If $t in.not phi.alt \( "Supp" G_(+) \)$, one may take $U$ disjoint from this closed image and
    set $Z_U = S = T = diameter$; the assertions below are then vacuous for the unique maps from the empty spaces.
    If $t in phi.alt \( "Supp" G_(+) \)$, there are a Hausdorff normal analytic space $Z_U$ of pure dimension $n$,
    considered near a reduced Cartier divisor $S$ of pure dimension $n - 1$,
    a reduced Weil divisor $T$ with no component in common with $S$, and maps
$ pi : Z_U arrow.r X \, wide g : S arrow.r U $

 with the following properties.]
  + #emph[The map $g$ is proper, and $pi \|_S$ is finite over $D_U = phi.alt^(- 1) \( U \)$, with $g =
    phi.alt pi \|_S$. Its image covers the positive components over $U$, and
$ cal(O)_S \( S \) tilde.eq g^(*) R_U . $

 The image of $S ∩ T$ lies in $phi.alt \( "Supp" G_(+) ∩ \( "Supp" G_(-) union D_0 \) \)$.
   Near $S$, the underlying sets satisfy
$ pi^(- 1) \( \| D \| \) = \| S \| union \| T \| . $

]
  + #emph[The pair $\( Z_U \, S + T \)$ is log canonical, and there is a fixed actual isomorphism of divisorial sheaves
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.5)")
 $ tau : cal(O)_(Z_U) \( a S \) arrow.r^(med tilde.eq med) omega_(Z_U) \( S + T \) . $ <signed:adjoint-chart>]

 The spaces $Z_U$ and $S$ are Cohen–Macaulay. The support of $T$ is locally set-theoretically principal.
   Off $T$, the space $Z_U$ is klt and its canonical sheaf is invertible. The map $pi$ is quasi-finite near $S \\ T$.]
  + #emph[Projective log resolutions of the total divisor data can be chosen with Kähler forms on neighborhoods of
    the compact sets over each compact fiber of $g$. Power-compatible changes of the boundary root extend to
    isomorphisms of ambient germs that preserve the full ideal $cal(O)_(Z_U) \( - S \)$, the reduced divisor $T$,
    and Equation (7.5). Resolutions may be chosen functorially for these germ isomorphisms.]
  #emph[Here the nonempty space $Z_U$ is a neighborhood of the whole compact fiber under consideration;
    no extension of $g$ to $Z_U$ is asserted.]
] <signed:root-charts>

#proof[
  The empty branch follows by shrinking away from the closed image $phi.alt \( "Supp" G_(+) \)$.
    We construct the nonempty charts in four stages: take roots and trivialize the remaining adjoint torsion,
    separate the positive and negative root divisors, retain the root line that controls the normal direction,
    and pass to an ordinary neighborhood of the compact fiber.
  First consider an analytic open set on which the Cartier lines of $m G_(+)$, $m G_(-)$, and $m D_0$ are trivial,
    and let $f_(+) \, f_(-) \, f_0$ be the functions representing their sections. Normalize the entire finite space
  $ { y_(+)^ell = f_(+) \, wide y_(-)^ell = f_(-) \, wide y_0^ell = f_0 } \, $
  retaining all its components and the lifted action of $mu_ell^3$ that multiplies the three root coordinates.
    On an overlap, the functions $f_(+) \, f_(-) \, f_0$ change by holomorphic units.
    Local $ell$-th roots of those units give comparisons of the finite spaces that lift uniquely to their
    normalizations; different choices differ by $mu_ell^3$. The quotient stacks of these normalized charts by
    $mu_ell^3$ glue to the simultaneous normalized root stack over $X$,
    which we denote by $cal(X)_(upright("rt"))$. The zero divisors of the three root sections are denoted by
    $S_(+) \, S_(-) \, S_0$. At a generic prime of multiplicity $b$, a normalized chart of $y^ell =
    x^b$ has ramification index $ell \/ b$ and $"ord" \( y \) = 1$.
    Here $b = \| m a_i \|$ or $b = m$, so it divides $ell$. Thus each root divisor is generically reduced and
    Cartier. A Cartier divisor on a normal space satisfies Serre’s condition $S_1$, so it is reduced.
    Log ramification with the full reduced boundary gives a log canonical pair and the rational equivalence
  $ K_(cal(X)_(upright("rt"))) + S_(+) + S_(-) + S_0 tilde.op_(bb(Q)) a \( S_(+) - S_(-) \) . $
  The ambient charts of $cal(X)_(upright("rt"))$ are klt. Off the boundary this follows from the klt complement
    downstairs and the unramified charts. For a place centered in the Cartier boundary,
    dropping that boundary increases its log discrepancy by its strictly positive order;
    this also makes every zero-discrepancy place positive.
  To turn the rational adjoint equivalence into an actual isomorphism,
    consider the integral reflexive sheaf on $cal(X)_(upright("rt"))$
  $ cal(F) = #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] omega_(cal(X)_(upright("rt"))) \( S_(+) + S_(-) + S_0 \) ⊗
    cal(O)_(cal(X)_(upright("rt"))) \( - a \( S_(+) - S_(-) \) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^(* *) . $
  It is torsion. Choose a periodicity $cal(F)^(\[ q \]) tilde.eq cal(O)$,
    take the relative spectrum of its reflexive-power algebra, and normalize;
    write $rho_(upright("ind"))$ for this finite map, and retain $S_(+) \, S_(-) \,
    S_0$ for the pulled-back root divisors on the cover. At codimension one this is a cover $w^q =
    u$ for a unit $u$, hence is unramified. Log canonicity, the klt ambient property,
    and reduced Cartier root divisors persist. The tautological evaluation of this algebra trivializes the
    reflexive pullback $\( rho_(upright("ind"))^(*) cal(F) \)^(* *)$:
    it does so in codimension one, and the identity then extends reflexively.
    The evaluation is a sheaf morphism on the cover stack itself, so the resulting actual divisorial isomorphism
  $ omega \( S_(+) + S_(-) + S_0 \) tilde.eq cal(O) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] a \( S_(+) - S_(-) \)
    #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] $
  on the cover is equivariant on every atlas.
  Next separate the positive and negative root divisors. Let $cal(X)_(upright("sep"))$ be the normalization of the
    blowup of the ideal generated by their equations on the cover, and write $p_1$ for its map.
    This blowup embeds in a relative $bb(P)^1$; its normalization still has fibers of dimension at most one.
    Each exceptional prime therefore lies over a codimension-two positive–negative intersection.
    At its generic point the original dlt pair is a two-branch SNC pair.
    After extracting roots of units the normalized Kummer chart there has smooth coordinates $x =
    u^(ell \/ b_(+))$, $z = w^(ell \/ b_(-))$, where $b_(+)$ and $b_(-)$ are the two corresponding multiplicities.
    On this chart $cal(F)$ is a line and its periodicity cover is unramified.
    The two-coordinate blowup calculation consequently applies at every exceptional generic point.
    If $E$ denotes its exceptional divisor, it gives reduced Cartier divisors
  $ E \, wide S'_(+) = p_1^(*) S_(+) - E \, wide S'_(-) = p_1^(*) S_(-) - E \, $
  with $S'_(+) ∩ S'_(-) = diameter$. No such two-branch stratum is contained in $S_0$,
    so $p_1^(*) S_0$ is reduced and has no exceptional component.
    The full boundary $S'_(+) + S'_(-) + E + p_1^(*) S_0$ is crepant and reduced.
    Its codimension-one equality with the pullback adjoint gives the discrepancy equality for every further
    valuation, hence log canonicity. The ambient charts after this blowup are still klt.
    Off the full boundary the blowup is an isomorphism to the old klt complement.
    A place of zero pair discrepancy centered in that boundary gains its strictly positive order when the
    effective Cartier boundary is dropped, while a place of positive pair discrepancy remains positive.
    The strict divisor $S'_(+)$ is finite over $S_(+)$: before normalization it lies in one section of the
    projective ratio coordinate, so its proper map has finite fibers, and normalization is finite.
  On $cal(X)_(upright("sep"))$, put $S = S'_(+)$ and, temporarily, $T = S'_(-) + E + p_1^(*) S_0$.
    Write $pi_(upright("sep")) : cal(X)_(upright("sep")) arrow.r X$ for the composite map,
    and define the retained root line
  $ cal(R) := cal(O)_(cal(X)_(upright("sep"))) \( S'_(+) - S'_(-) \) \, wide
    cal(R)^ell tilde.eq pi_(upright("sep"))^(*) N_0 . $
  Near $S$, the negative strict root section is a unit. The fixed adjoint isomorphism becomes
    $omega_(cal(X)_(upright("sep"))) \( S + T \) tilde.eq cal(O)_(cal(X)_(upright("sep"))) \( a S \)$,
    and the positive root section divided by that negative unit section is a section of $cal(R)$ with zero divisor
    $S$. This line and section determine the Cartier divisor $S$ and its normal line;
    they must survive passage to an ordinary chart.
  The pair consisting of $cal(R)$ and its displayed power isomorphism defines a map to the gerbe $root(ell, N_0)$,
    which parametrizes $ell$-th roots of $N_0$. Take the relative coarse space over this gerbe.
    On an atlas this quotients by the finite relative inertia kernel,
    the subgroup of each stabilizer acting trivially on $cal(R)$.
    Thus the quotient retains the root character of $cal(R)$.
    The deck transformations of the representable periodicity cover are not this inertia kernel and are not removed.
  Both $cal(R)$ and its section descend through the kernel,
    so the reduced image $S$ remains Cartier and its full ideal descends.
    We retain $S \, T$ for the reduced images. A finite-group norm of a local equation of the upstairs $T$ has
    precisely its image as zero set. Thus the reduced image $T$ is locally set-theoretically principal;
    it need not be Cartier. All divisorial ramification lies on the full boundary.
    Log ramification identifies the invariant log dualizing sheaf in codimension one with the downstairs one.
    Taking reflexive hulls and invariants of the equivariant adjoint isomorphism gives an actual descended
    isomorphism $cal(O) \( a S \) tilde.eq omega \( S + T \)$. Its ordinary pullback below is Equation (7.5).
    Outside $S + T$, the local finite quotient is quasi-étale,
    so its klt upstairs complement gives a klt downstairs complement by finite discrepancy comparison.
    Away from $T$, dropping the Cartier divisor $S$ from the lc pair increases the log discrepancy of every place
    centered in $S$ by its strictly positive order. Thus the quotient charts are klt away from $T$,
    and the same isomorphism makes their canonical sheaves invertible there.
    Off $T$ the separating blowup is an isomorphism, while the other operations are finite;
    hence the composite map to $X$ is quasi-finite near $S \\ T$.
  The same quotient preserves Cohen–Macaulayness of both the ambient space and $S$.
    Before the quotient the ambient space is klt and hence Cohen–Macaulay, and $S$ is Cartier there.
    Locally its finite quotient ring, and the quotient ring of $S$,
    are invariant direct summands of those finite Cohen–Macaulay rings.
    A system of parameters downstairs is one upstairs; the vanishing of lower local cohomology upstairs and the
    invariant projection give its vanishing for the downstairs direct summand. This proves the stated depth.
  Removal of the relative inertia kernel also makes the strict positive divisor finite and representable over
  $ D times_P root(ell, cal(O)_P \( 1 \)) . $
  Its root line is $cal(O)_S \( S \)$, and its image covers $"Supp" G_(+)$.
    The intersection $S ∩ T$ maps to the positive–negative or positive–zero intersections:
    the only new component is the separating exceptional divisor.
    Before the quotient, the inverse image of $\| D \|$ is the support of $S'_(+) + S'_(-) + E + p_1^(*) S_0$.
    The finite quotient sends this full support to $\| S \| union \| T \|$,
    and the equality of underlying sets persists under the ordinary base change below.
    The image of $S ∩ T$ in $P$ consequently has dimension $< r$, by Lemma 97.
  Finally we realize these stack data on an ordinary analytic neighborhood of the whole compact fiber.
    We use the dimension-free root-neighborhood result #cite(label("BL")) Lemma 10.1.
    For a compact analytic subspace $F$ of a Hausdorff analytic space,
    that lemma extends a specified $ell$-th root of a line on $F$ to a root on a neighborhood.
    It also extends a specified power-compatible comparison of two roots uniquely as a germ about $F$.
    Its proof is the analytic Kummer sequence and continuity of the cohomology of $mu_ell$ over neighborhoods of a
    compact set, in degrees two, one, and zero.
  Apply it to $F = phi.alt^(- 1) \( t \)_(upright("red")) subset
    X$ with the root prescribed by $R_U$ in Equation (7.1). Apply the comparison clause also inside $D$.
    Properness of $phi.alt$ allows a shrink of $U$ for which the comparison is defined along all of $D_U$:
    remove the closed image of its complement. Pulling the relative coarse construction across this root over the
    whole neighborhood of $F$ gives the ordinary Hausdorff space $Z_U$.
    Here the chosen root defines a map from that neighborhood to $root(ell, N_0)$,
    and the relative quotient is representable over the gerbe, so this pullback is an ordinary space.
    Finite invariant algebras glue its local quotient charts.
    The preceding finite representable map becomes the finite map $S arrow.r D_U$, so $g$ is proper.
    The retained line on $S$ becomes $g^(*) R_U$, giving $cal(O)_S \( S \) tilde.eq g^(*) R_U$.
    Normalization and the periodicity cover are finite also in the analytic category.
    The separating blowup is projective. A common power of its equivariant relative ample line kills the bounded
    finite stabilizer characters and descends to the coarse quotient;
    relative ampleness is checked on fibers after the finite pullback.
    Thus these operations and a projective log resolution admit relative ample metrics.
    Adding a sufficiently large pullback of the ambient Kähler form gives Kähler forms near the compact sets in
    question. Finally, power-compatible root comparisons identify the full constructions as ambient germs.
    Smooth-functorial projective resolution of the same ordered divisor data preserves those identifications.
    This proves all assertions. ◻
]

The residue assertions below are vacuous for an empty chart.
  Fix a nonempty chart of Lemma 98, write $Z = Z_U$, and choose a projective log resolution $p : hat(Z) arrow.r
  Z$. Write

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.6)")
 $ D_S = p^(*) S \, wide H = \( D_S \)_(upright("red")) \, wide C = \( p^(- 1) T \)_(upright("red")) \,\
A = sum_(E upright(" component of ") C\
E upright(" not a component of ") H) E . $ <signed:resolved-divisors>]

