=== 7.3 The localized Hodge argument <the-localized-hodge-argument>

The target in Equation (7.7) contains the additional poles $A$.
  The following version of the filtered SNC calculation incorporates them.
  In its application, we embed $H$ by the graph of $h$ in $hat(Z) times
  U$ and use the projection to $U$ as the ambient map. This projection is submersive,
  while its restriction to the graph is the proper map $h$; it requires no extension of $h$ to $hat(Z)$.
  We will use the same graph construction after a change of parameter.
  The parameter is projective only in the last assertion below; the closed-stratum maps are proper Kähler maps.

#thm([Proposition 7.5.])[
  #emph[Let $q :  V arrow.r Y$ be a holomorphic map of complex manifolds,
    submersive near a reduced closed analytic subspace $i : H
    arrow.r.hook  V$ of pure dimension $d$ and codimension $c_0 > 0$. Suppose $h = q \|_H$ is proper.
    Assume that locally $H$ is an SNC divisor in a smooth submanifold of codimension $c_0 - 1$,
    and that a reduced divisor $A$ is simultaneously SNC and transverse to those support equations.
    Assume that the components $H_i$ and $A_j$ near $H$ have finite global smooth indexing,
    and that every closed stratum $H_I ∩ A_J$, with $I eq.not diameter$,
    is smooth and proper over $Y$ and carries a global relative Kähler form.
    Empty or disconnected strata are allowed. Here $H_I = ⋂_(i in I) H_i$ and $A_J = ⋂_(j in J) A_j$,
    with $A_diameter =  V$.]
  #emph[Use right $ D$-modules and finite-pole local cohomology, and put
$ cal(K)_A = cal(H)_(\[ H \])^(c_0) \( omega_( V) \) \( * A \) . $

 Its order filtration is generated from $F_0 cal(K)_A = i_(*) omega_H \( A \)$, with $F_p = 0$ for $p < 0$.
   For $M^j = cal(H)^j q_(+) cal(K)_A$, the filtered direct image is strict at every level,
   and $M^j$ has a finite filtration by submodules, strict for $F$,
   whose quotients are polarizable real pure Hodge modules. In particular,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.11)")
 $ F_0 M^j = R^j h_(*) omega_H \( A \) arrow.r.hook M^j \, wide F_p M^j = 0 quad \( p < 0 \) . $ <signed:lowest-step>]

 Define the symbol morphism
$ sigma : F_0 M^j ⊗ T_Y arrow.r "gr"_1^F M^j \, wide sigma \( m ⊗ xi \) = \[ m xi \] . $

 If $Y$ is smooth projective, then for every ample line $N$ on $Y$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.12)")
 $ "Hom"_(cal(O)_Y) \( N \, ker sigma \) = 0 . $ <signed:symbol-vanishing>]

 This includes any coherent torsion in the kernel.]
] <signed:localized-hodge>

#proof[
  We verify the residual localization in the proof of #cite(label("BL")) Proposition 11.1.
    We first identify the lowest order step and a filtration by the number of branch poles.
    Its graded pieces will be dual constant modules on smooth strata, to which proper Kähler direct image applies.
    The resulting strictness gives the injection at the lowest filtration step,
    and the graded de Rham complex on a projective base gives the symbol vanishing.
  At a point of $H$, choose simultaneous coordinates
  $ \( z_1 \, dots.h \, z_(c_0 - 1) \, x_1 \, dots.h \, x_t \, y_1 \, dots.h \, y_s \, xi \) \,
    quad  I_H = \( z_1 \, dots.h \, z_(c_0 - 1) \, x_1 dots.h.c x_t \) \, quad A = \( y_1 dots.h.c y_s = 0 \) . $
  Let $eta$ be the coordinate volume form. The localization Čech complex for the displayed regular support
    equations has cohomology only in degree $c_0$. Localizing this cohomology also in the $y$’s gives $cal(K)_A$.
    Successive finite Taylor divisions give its unique additive polar normal forms: finite sums of
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.13)")
   $ f \( x_(I^c) \, y_(J^c) \, xi \) product_(nu = 1)^(c_0 - 1) z_nu^(- u_nu) product_(i in I) x_i^(- v_i)
     product_(j in J) y_j^(- w_j) eta \, quad I eq.not diameter \, $ <signed:polar-form>]
  where every displayed order is positive and $J$ is arbitrary, including empty.
    The coefficient is independent of exactly the variables occurring negatively.
    The normal form is an additive statement, not an $cal(O)_( V)$-linear decomposition.
  The simple fractions, with each displayed order equal to one, are exactly the image of $omega_H \( A \)$.
    More explicitly, their unreduced representatives are
  $ frac(f eta, z_1 dots.h.c z_(c_0 - 1) \( x_1 dots.h.c x_t \) \( y_1 dots.h.c y_s \)) . $
  The regular-equation residue identifies their numerators modulo $ I_H$ with the absolute dualizing sheaf of $H$,
    twisted by $A$. This map is injective. If the fraction has no remaining $x$-pole,
    Taylor division says that its numerator modulo the $z$’s is divisible by $x_1 dots.h.c x_t$.
    Localization in the $y$’s does not enlarge this kernel, because each $y_j$ is a nonzerodivisor modulo $ I_H$.
    This also proves the identification under changes of coordinates,
    including the determinant of the normal conormal frame.
  Give a term of Equation (7.13) the excess
  $ e = sum_nu \( u_nu - 1 \) + sum_(i in I) \( v_i - 1 \) + sum_(j in J) \( w_j - 1 \) . $
  The right canonical action of a coordinate derivative is minus differentiation of the coefficient of $eta$.
    A derivative in a denominator variable increases its order by one with a nonzero scalar.
    Because the coefficient in the normal form is independent of those variables,
    every term of excess $e$ is obtained from a simple term by $e$ such derivatives.
    Conversely, order $p$ differentiations produce excess at most $p$.
    Thus $F_p cal(K)_A$ consists exactly of the finite sums of excess at most $p$.
    This is the good order filtration generated from $i_(*) omega_H \( A \)$.
  There is also an increasing filtration $W$ by the total number of branch poles.
    Intrinsically, its step of index $- d + k - 1$ is the sum of the images of support modules for subunions of
    the $H_i$, localized along sublists of the $A_j$, for which the total number of selected branches is at most
    $k$. In the normal form it imposes $\| I \| + \| J \| lt.eq k$. The induced double filtration therefore has
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.14)")
   $ "gr"_(- d + k - 1)^W \( cal(K)_A \, F \) tilde.eq xor.big_(\| I \| + \| J \| = k\
I eq.not diameter) \( i_(I \, J) \)_(+) \( omega_(H_I ∩ A_J) \, F^(\( 0 \)) \) . $ <signed:localized-graded>]
  Here $F_p^(\( 0 \)) omega_(H_I ∩ A_J) = 0$ for $p < 0$ and is $omega_(H_I ∩ A_J)$ for $p gt.eq 0$.
    Here $A_diameter =  V$, and empty intersections contribute zero.
    To check this formula, retain the polar type using exactly $I \, J$.
    Its normal denominators are the $c_0 - 1$ equations $z_nu$, the $\| I \|$ selected $x$’s,
    and the $\| J \|$ selected $y$’s. They are the regular equations of the smooth stratum $H_I ∩ A_J$,
    whose dimension is $d - k + 1$. Right closed transfer has no codimension shift in $F$,
    and the excess counts exactly its normal derivative orders.
    Mayer–Vietoris boundary maps for the $x$-subunions and the localization boundary for each selected $y$
    identify these quotients intrinsically with the support module of that intersection.
    For the latter assertion, localization modulo the nonlocalized module in one transverse $y$ direction is its
    first local cohomology in that direction. A fixed order of the component labels fixes the residue signs,
    so the identifications glue. The smooth- support graded modules are regular holonomic,
    and their finite extension $cal(K)_A$ is regular holonomic as well.
  The filtration $W$ has a real realization. Tensor the finite-pole de Rham comparisons for the regular support
    coordinates with the open localization comparison for the $y$ coordinates,
    whose one-coordinate factor is the cohomology of a punctured disk.
    These comparisons are natural for all sublists. The resulting real support and localization complexes
    complexify to the de Rham complexes just computed. The latter are perverse by regular holonomicity;
    exact and faithful complexification gives the same assertion for the real complexes.
    Their real perverse images then give $W$. On a graded stratum of dimension $d - k + 1$,
    the real object is its dual constant object $bb(R)^H \[ d - k + 1 \] \( d - k + 1 \)$.
    Its first right filtration step is zero and its weight is $- d + k - 1$, in agreement with Equation (7.14).
    Residue signs and constant normalizations preserve its real polarization.
    This is the real comparison in #cite(label("BL")) Section 11.2,
    with the punctured-disk factors supplying the additional localization boundaries.
  The proper-support check also survives localization. If a holomorphic germ $f$ vanishes on reduced $H$, then
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.15)")
   $ f F_p cal(K)_A subset F_(p - 1) cal(K)_A . $ <signed:support-condition>]
  For a generator $z_nu$ of $ I_H$, multiplication reduces its pole order or kills the class.
    Multiplication by $x_1 dots.h.c x_t$ also lowers the excess or kills the class,
    because the polar type always has $I eq.not diameter$. The $y$-poles do not affect this argument.
    In particular every graded stratum stays in the proper support $H$, and properness is needed only there.
  We have now identified the order filtration, its real pure stratum pieces, and the proper support of each level.
    We next pass these data to the base. Apply Saito’s constant-source Kähler direct-image theorem
    #cite(label("SaitoKahler")) Theorem 1 and Remark 1.2 to each smooth closed stratum in Equation (7.14),
    using its stipulated relative Kähler form. The resulting degree-$j$ direct image of the step indexed by
    $lambda$ is strict and polarizable real pure of weight $lambda + j$.
    In the submersion neighborhood, the level-$F_p$ relative right de Rham complex has term
  $ F_(p - e) cal(K)_A ⊗ and.big^e T_( V \/ Y) quad upright("in degree ") - e . $
  The simultaneous excess and branch bounds show that passing to a $W$-quotient commutes with taking each such level.
  To prove strictness, use the finite $W$ spectral sequence
  $ E_1^(- lambda \, j + lambda) = cal(H)^j q_(+) "gr"_lambda^W cal(K)_A quad arrow.r.double quad
    cal(H)^j q_(+) cal(K)_A . $
  Its first-page terms and their level filtrations are strict pure objects by the preceding application.
    The differentials are real and filtration preserving, because the support and localization comparisons are
    real and natural. The first differential preserves weight, hence is a strict morphism of pure Hodge modules;
    its kernels and cokernels remain pure. A higher differential sends $\( lambda \, j \)$ to
    $\( lambda - k \, j + 1 \)$ for $k gt.eq 2$ and strictly lowers weight. Such a real filtered map is zero.
    On distinct strict supports this follows from strict support;
    on the same dense smooth support a real map preserves both Hodge filtrations,
    and a source vector of type $\( p \, q \)$ would have image in $F^p ∩
    overline(F)^q$ of a pure object of smaller weight, which is zero.
    Both the unfiltered and each level spectral sequence therefore degenerate at the second page.
    Their comparison is injective there. A least-$W$-step argument on the finite abutment proves injection at
    every $F$ level and strictness of its induced $W$ filtration.
    This is the strict-direct-image argument of #cite(label("BL")) Proposition 11.1,
    now justified for every graded term with $A$-poles.
  For $p < 0$, the relative complex is zero. For $p = 0$, its only term is $F_0 cal(K)_A =
    i_(*) omega_H \( A \)$ in degree zero. The level injection gives Equation (7.11).
  Suppose finally that $Y$ is smooth projective. Each strict- support pure quotient just obtained is of exactly
    the type in #cite(label("BL")) Lemma 11.2: a polarizable real pure direct-image piece arising from a dual
    constant on a smooth Kähler source. That lemma compares its actual right filtration with the Sabbah–Schnell
    pure component extending the same generic polarized real variation.
    Its proof uses uniqueness of the intermediate extension and the reconstruction of $F$ from the canonical
    $V$-filtration, so it depends only on this pure piece, not on the divisor presentation of its source.
    It gives the negative-ample vanishing
  $ bb(H)^u #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] Y \, N^(- 1) ⊗ "gr"_p^F upright("DR")_Y Q #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] =
    0 quad \( u < 0 \) $
  for each pure quotient $Q$, by #cite(label("SabbahSchnell")) Theorem 16.3.10.
    The finite strict filtration extends this vanishing to $M^j$.
    Since its negative $F$ levels vanish, its degree-one graded right de Rham complex is exactly
  $ "gr"_1^F upright("DR")_Y M^j = [F_0 M^j ⊗ T_Y arrow.r^sigma "gr"_1^F M^j] quad upright("in degrees ") - 1 \, 0 . $
  Its negative hypercohomology after tensoring by $N^(- 1)$ is $H^0 \( Y \, N^(- 1) ⊗ ker sigma \)$.
    It vanishes, which is Equation (7.12). This calculation does not assume that the kernel is locally free. ◻
]

=== 7.4 Lifting every finite boundary neighborhood <lifting-every-finite-boundary-neighborhood>

Return to the charts and resolution of Lemma 98 and Equation (7.6). On an empty chart the sheaves below are zero.
  On a nonempty chart put $I = cal(O)_Z \( - S \)$, an invertible ideal.
  For every integer $j$ and positive integer $k$, put

$ cal(A)_j = I^j \/ I^(j + 1) = cal(O)_S \( - j S \) \, wide cal(T)_(j \, k) = I^j \/ I^(j + k) . $

These are sheaves on the underlying topological space of $S$.
  Pushforward by $g$ has that meaning for $cal(T)_(j \, k)$;
  it does not require a morphism from a thickening to $U$. A local frame of $R_U^(- 1)$ gives a Laurent graded
  frame $u^j$ of $cal(A)_j$ for every $j$. It is a frame on the associated graded, not an extension of a frame to $Z$.

#thm([Proposition 7.6.])[
  (All finite lifting orders).  #emph[For every root chart, every $j in bb(Z)$, and every $k gt.eq 1$,
    the map of sheaves of complex vector spaces on $U$
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.16)")
 $ g_(*) cal(T)_(j \, k + 1) arrow.r g_(*) cal(T)_(j \, k) $ <signed:all-order-surjection>]

 is surjective. The assertion holds on every parameter germ, including germs supported at special parameters.]
] <signed:all-orders>

#proof[
  The assertion is immediate for an empty chart. We induct on $k$,
    simultaneously for all integer $j$ and all nonempty charts.
    The layer algebra and its connecting maps are the ones in #cite(label("BL")) Lemma 12.1;
    we recall their construction to specify exactly which obstruction is to be killed.
