=== 7.5 Compact deformations and the signed theorem <compact-deformations-and-the-signed-theorem>

We now turn the infinitesimal lifting into actual compact subspaces.
  The role of all the integer layers in Proposition 101 is that both the equations of a boundary fiber and a
  generator of its normal direction can be lifted compatibly.
  This is the analytic counterpart of the compact-family step in #cite(label("LI")) Proposition 5.1.

#thm([Lemma 7.7.])[
  #emph[Assume $0 < v < n$, set $d = n - v$, and choose a positive component $D_i$ with $dim phi.alt \( D_i \) =
    r$. There is a nonempty open subset $D_i^compose subset D_i$ with the following property.
    For every $x in D_i^compose$, there are a disk $Delta$ about $0$, a proper flat analytic family $ F arrow.r
    Delta$ of compact subspaces of pure dimension $d$, and a finite morphism
$ F arrow.r X times Delta $

 over $Delta$, such that every nonzero fiber has image disjoint from $D$,
   while $x$ is a limit of points in those images as the parameter tends to zero.
   Its schematic image is flat over the disk with pure $d$-dimensional fibers,
   whose fundamental cycles form a bounded family in the cycle space of $X$ after shrinking the disk.]
] <signed:escaping-fibers>

#proof[
  Choose a general point of the reduced image $phi.alt \( D_i \)$,
    outside the small image in Equation (7.2) and the intersections with distinct component images.
    Work on one ordinary root chart over a neighborhood $U$ of that point.
    Its finite map $S arrow.r D_U$ has a component covering a nonempty open of $D_i ∩ D_U$.
    Shrink to a smooth open $Y$ of $phi.alt \( D_i \) ∩ U$, remove the images of components that do not dominate
    it, and remove the nonflat locus of the proper map $g$. The last removal is proper by analytic generic
    flatness. The relevant $S$ is now flat over the smooth $r$-fold $Y$, and its fibers avoid $T$.
    They are compact of pure dimension $n - 1 - r = d > 0$. On the selected component the locus where it is
    singular or where $g$ has rank less than $r$ is proper. Removing its image,
    along with the preceding proper bad subsets, under the finite map leaves a nonempty open $D_i^compose subset
    D_i$. Its points have preimages at smooth points of these fiber components.
  Fix $x in D_i^compose$ and a point above it in the compact fiber $F = g^(- 1) \( t \)$, for $t in Y$.
    Work on the single ordinary chart neighborhood of that fiber from Lemma 98.
    Take regular coordinates $t_1 \, dots.h \, t_r$ on $Y$ centered at $t$,
    extended to local coordinates of its smooth embedding in $P$.
    Their pullbacks form a regular sequence on $S$ along $F$, by flatness.
    In addition, $S$ is Cohen–Macaulay, so $F$ is Cohen–Macaulay of pure dimension $d$.
  Choose once and for all a Hausdorff open neighborhood $W subset Z$ of $F$, disjoint from $T$,
    on which $pi$ is quasi-finite. Set $I = cal(O)_Z \( - S \)$.
    Proposition 101 lets us lift the finitely many $t_i$’s to compatible germs of sections of $cal(O)_Z \/ I^q$,
    $q gt.eq 1$, along $F$. It also lifts the conormal frame $u in I \/ I^2$ to compatible germs $tilde(y)_q in
    I \/ I^(q + 1)$ along $F$; write $tilde(y)$ for this system.
    At each order choose simultaneous representatives on a neighborhood of $F$ contained in $W$.
    These neighborhoods may shrink with the order. The leading coefficient of $tilde(y)$ is a unit frame,
    so it generates $I$ on each finite thickening.
  For $R_q = bb(C) \[ s \] \/ \( s^q \)$, cut the lifted $r$ equations in the Cartier thickening $q S$,
    and map $s$ to $tilde(y)$. Denote the resulting subspace by $F_q$.
    It is flat over the Artin analytic point $"Spec" R_q$, with closed fiber $F$.
    Before cutting, multiplication by the Cartier generator identifies each successive $s$-layer with $cal(O)_S$;
    this is the flatness criterion over $R_q$. Quotienting by lifts of the closed-fiber regular sequence preserves
    flatness, by the local flatness criterion. Compatibility of the lifted equations gives compatible reductions
    of all $F_q$.
  Each of these is an embedded compact deformation in the same fixed neighborhood $W$.
    To see why the shrinking representative neighborhoods cause no difficulty,
    the support of $F_q$ is the fixed compact set $F$. Its ideal on its representative neighborhood glues with the
    unit ideal on $W \\ F$, since the deformation is empty on their overlap away from $F$.
    This realizes it as a closed subspace of $W times "Spec" R_q$.
    The ideal is coherent locally on both opens, and the family is proper over the Artin point because its support
    is compact.
  The Douady space of compact subspaces of $W$ represents proper flat embedded families #cite(label("Douady1966"))
    Section 9. The compatible $F_q$’s thus define a formal arc in its analytic germ at $\[ F \]$.
    Embed that germ in a finite dimensional analytic coordinate space.
    The arc is a formal solution of its convergent defining equations.
    Analytic Artin approximation #cite(label("Artin1968")) Theorem 1.2 gives a convergent arc agreeing modulo
    $s^2$. Pull back the universal family and shrink its disk to obtain $ F subset W times Delta$,
    proper and flat over $Delta$, with closed fiber $F$ and the prescribed first normal displacement.
  The family may be taken to have pure $d$-dimensional fibers throughout the disk. Here is a local justification.
    The central fiber is Cohen–Macaulay of dimension $d$. Flatness over the regular one-dimensional base makes its
    parameter a nonzerodivisor and gives the depth and dimension of the total local ring as $d +
    1$ along that fiber. Cohen–Macaulayness is open for analytic local rings,
    and properness allows a disk shrink for which it holds on the whole family.
    Every total component then meets the central fiber after shrinking, and flatness makes it dominate the disk;
    the dimension formula gives it dimension $d + 1$. Quotient by the nonzerodivisor at each parameter gives
    Cohen–Macaulay fibers of dimension $d$. The analytic dimension formula and unmixedness of these local rings
    give the asserted purity.
  Let $f$ be a local equation of $S$. On $ F$ it vanishes on the central fiber, so flatness writes it as $f =
    s h$. Agreement modulo $s^2$ with the formal deformation says that $h \|_F$ is a unit:
    this is precisely the lifted conormal generator. Finitely many such neighborhoods cover the compact $F$.
    Properness of $ F arrow.r Delta$ lets us shrink the disk so that they cover the entire family and all their
    $h$’s are units; remove the closed image of the complement. Thus a nonzero fiber misses $S$.
    The neighborhood $W$ already misses $T$, and the set equality in Lemma 98 gives $pi^(- 1) \( \| D \| \) ∩
    W = \| S \| ∩ W$. Hence the nonzero fiber images miss $D$.
  The induced map $Pi :  F arrow.r X times Delta$ is proper:
    the source is proper over $Delta$ and the target is Hausdorff over it,
    so the graph is closed and the projection is proper. It is quasi-finite by the choice of $W$, hence finite.
    It therefore preserves the dimension of every fiber component.
    Every total component through a central point dominates the disk by flatness, and its punctured part is dense.
    Thus every central point, including the point chosen above $x$,
    is a limit of points of nonzero fibers of dimension $d$.
  Let $ Z subset X times Delta$ be the schematic image of this finite map.
    Its algebra is the coherent image of $cal(O)_(X times Delta) arrow.r Pi_(*) cal(O)_( F)$.
    Multiplication by a nonzero germ from the disk is injective on $cal(O)_( F)$, by flatness,
    and remains injective after the exact finite pushforward. It is therefore injective on the image algebra.
    That algebra is torsion-free, hence flat, over each local discrete valuation ring of the disk.
    Thus $ Z$ is a proper flat family of subspaces of $X$. The finite map to its schematic image is surjective,
    also on each fiber as a map of supports. Each $ Z_s$ consequently has pure dimension $d$.
    The Douady-to-cycle morphism, in the form recorded in #cite(label("Fujiki1978")) Section 3.3,
    makes their fundamental cycles an analytic family in the cycle space of $X$.
    The restriction to a smaller closed disk is compact. The volumes of these cycles against a Kähler form are
    bounded by continuity, proving the last assertion. ◻
]

#proof[
  The assertion is immediate in dimension zero, and the case $D = 0$ follows directly from the signed equivalence.
    By Lemma 97, the case $v = 0$ already gives $L tilde.op_(bb(Q)) 0$, and $v = n > 0$ contradicts $a \( X \) =
    0$. Suppose, therefore, that $0 < v < n$. Choose the positive $D_i$ in that lemma and put $d = n - v$,
    so $0 < d < n$. We will contradict simplicity by using the compact deformations in Lemma 102.
  Let $cal(C)_d \( X \)$ be the Barlet space of nonzero effective compact $d$-cycles on $X$.
    It is a second-countable analytic space, hence has only countably many irreducible components $B_alpha$.
    For compact Kähler $X$ its connected components, and therefore its irreducible components,
    are compact #cite(label("Fujiki1978")) Theorem 4.5. The universal support $cal(U)_alpha subset B_alpha times
    X$ is a compact analytic subspace. Take all of its full irreducible components $V_(alpha beta)$ that contain
    an incidence point belonging to an integral $d$-cycle disjoint from $D$.
    There are countably many of these components, since each compact analytic $cal(U)_alpha$ has finitely many.
    Their evaluation images
  $ W_(alpha beta) = "pr"_X \( V_(alpha beta) \) $
  are closed irreducible analytic subspaces of $X$, by proper mapping. Each has a point outside $D$.
    We use the full compact components here, including their fibers at limiting cycle parameters;
    the incidence over only the open set of cycles avoiding $D$ would not have a proper evaluation map.
  These countably many closed images cover $D_i^compose$. Indeed, for $x in D_i^compose$,
    choose the family in Lemma 102 and a sequence $x_j arrow.r x$ in nonzero fiber images.
    Let $C_j$ be an integral component of the fundamental cycle of the schematic image fiber containing $x_j$,
    taken with coefficient one. The cycles $C_j$ avoid $D$, and their volumes are bounded by the volumes of the
    total image cycles. Bounded cycles on compact $X$ have relatively compact closure in $cal(C)_d \( X \)$
    #cite(label("Fujiki1978")) Proposition 2.10. The irreducible components of an analytic space are locally
    finite, so this compact closure meets only finitely many $B_alpha$.
    After passing to a subsequence, the cycles lie in a fixed $B_alpha$; after a second subsequence,
    the points $\( C_j \, x_j \)$ lie in a fixed full component $V_(alpha beta)$.
    Its evaluation image is closed, so it contains $x$. This proves the covering assertion.
  The analytic space $D_i^compose$ is locally compact and Baire.
    If no $W_(alpha beta)$ contained $D_i$, each intersection with $D_i^compose$ would be a proper closed analytic
    subset and hence nowhere dense. The countable cover just proved contradicts Baire.
    Consequently some $W_(alpha beta)$ contains $D_i$. It also contains a point outside $D$.
    An irreducible proper analytic subspace of the irreducible $n$-fold $X$ that contains the prime divisor $D_i$
    must equal $D_i$, by dimension. It follows that $W_(alpha beta) = X$.
  Every point of this full incidence component lies on the support of the effective $d$-cycle at its parameter,
    including limiting parameters. Thus its surjective evaluation supplies through every point of $X$ an
    irreducible compact component of such a cycle. Each has dimension $d$, strictly between zero and $n$.
    This contradicts simplicity. The intermediate range for $v$ is impossible, leaving $L tilde.op_(bb(Q)) 0$.
    By the meaning of rational linear equivalence for the actual line $L$,
    a positive Cartier multiple is the trivial holomorphic line. ◻
]

=== 7.6 The simple case of the induction <the-simple-case-of-the-induction>

We now pass from the original pair to a reduced signed boundary and a nef model,
  and then subtract the added boundary from the torsion comparison.

#proof[
  Let $\( X \, B \)$ and $J = K_X + B$ be as in that proposition.
    A positive-dimensional compact complex curve is projective, so $a \( X \) = 0$ implies $n gt.eq 2$.
    By Theorem 86, a positive multiple of $K_X$ has a nonzero meromorphic section.
    It gives an actual rational equivalence $K_X tilde.op_(bb(Q)) G_K$ for a signed rational divisor $G_K$.
  Choose a projective log resolution $p_0 : W arrow.r X$ of the boundary and the support of this divisor.
    Let $B_W$ be the strict transform of $B$ together with every new exceptional prime with coefficient one.
    The resolution transfer in Proposition 9 gives the actual rational identity
  $ K_W + B_W tilde.op_(bb(Q)) p_0^(*) J + E_0 \, wide E_0 gt.eq 0 quad upright("exceptional over ") X . $
  The canonical pullback formula transforms $G_K$ to a signed representative of $K_W$.
    Enlarge the reduced support of $B_W$ and this representative to a reduced SNC divisor $D$. Then
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.24)")
   $ J_D := K_W + D tilde.op_(bb(Q)) p_0^(*) J + E_0 + \( D - B_W \) \, wide E_0 + \( D - B_W \) gt.eq
     0 . $ <signed:enlarged-adjoint>]
  In particular $J_D$ is pseudo-effective and has a signed rational representative supported on $D$.
    Simplicity and algebraic dimension zero persist under these birational modifications.
  Apply Corollary 29 to $\( W \, D \)$ and $J_D$. Equation (7.24) supplies pseudo-effectivity,
    and the signed representative is supported on the floor $D$.
    Denote the resulting ordinary dlt nef model by $\( X_(upright("nef")) \, D_(upright("nef")) \)$,
    and put $L_(upright("nef")) = K_(X_(upright("nef"))) + D_(upright("nef"))$.
    As a working model of the program, $X_(upright("nef"))$ is globally strongly $bb(Q)$-factorial,
    and the pair has the resolution property of Definition 16.
    The space $X_(upright("nef"))$ is simple and compact Kähler with $a \( X_(upright("nef")) \) = 0$;
    $D_(upright("nef"))$ is reduced, $L_(upright("nef"))$ is nef,
    and its actual signed representative is supported on $D_(upright("nef"))$.
    Theorem 66 makes $L_(upright("nef")) \|_(D_(upright("nef")))$ semiample on the whole reduced floor.
  We check the remaining pseudo-effectivity hypothesis of Theorem 96, with exactly $c = 1$.
    Take a common projective resolution $alpha : V arrow.r W$, $beta : V arrow.r X_(upright("nef"))$,
    with $V$ smooth compact Kähler. The manifold $V$ is still simple, hence not uniruled.
    Ou’s criterion #cite(label("Ou2025")) Theorem 1.1 makes $K_V$ pseudo-effective;
    this implication uses no assumption about the absence of canonical sections.
    Since $X_(upright("nef"))$ is globally strongly $bb(Q)$-factorial,
    $K_(X_(upright("nef")))$ is a rational line. Write its canonical comparison as an identity of actual rational lines
  $ K_V tilde.op_(bb(Q)) beta^(*) K_(X_(upright("nef"))) + F_K \, wide F_K = F_K^(+) - F_K^(-) \, $
  where both $F_K^(+)$ and $F_K^(-)$ are effective and $beta$-exceptional.
    Thus $beta^(*) { K_(X_(upright("nef"))) } + { F_K^(+) } = { K_V } + { F_K^(-) }$ is pseudo-effective.
    Exceptional translation in Lemma 5 removes the effective $beta$-exceptional $F_K^(+)$ and proves that
    $beta^(*) { K_(X_(upright("nef"))) }$ is pseudo-effective.
    Since $L_(upright("nef")) - D_(upright("nef")) = K_(X_(upright("nef")))$ as an actual rational line,
    this is precisely the resolution hypothesis for $L_(upright("nef")) - D_(upright("nef"))$, with $c = 1$.
    Theorem 96 now gives $L_(upright("nef")) tilde.op_(bb(Q)) 0$.
  On the same common resolution, enlarging it if necessary, the accumulated negative-step comparisons give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.25)")
   $ alpha^(*) J_D tilde.op_(bb(Q)) beta^(*) L_(upright("nef")) + F \, wide F gt.eq 0 quad
     upright("exceptional over ") X_(upright("nef")) . $ <signed:program-decomposition>]
  All identities here are identities of actual rational lines.
    Because $L_(upright("nef"))$ is torsion, its pullback is nef with zero class.
    Lemma 5 therefore identifies $F = N \( alpha^(*) J_D \)$.
  Put $mu = p_0 alpha : V arrow.r X$ and $Q = alpha^(*) \( E_0 + D - B_W \) gt.eq 0$.
    Pulling up Equation (7.24) gives $mu^(*) J tilde.op_(bb(Q)) alpha^(*) J_D - Q$, which is pseudo-effective.
    Apply Lemma 8 to Equation (7.25). A positive current for $mu^(*) J$, plus $\[ Q \]$,
    must be the unique current $\[ F \]$ in the larger adjoint class.
    It follows that $Q lt.eq F$, and negative-part subtraction gives the actual identity
  $ mu^(*) J tilde.op_(bb(Q)) F - Q = N \( mu^(*) J \) . $
  The divisor on the right is effective and rational. This is exactly Proposition 14 and the required instance of
    $cal(G)_n$ with torsion positive part. ◻
]

